var e=[{topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Pharmacognosy is the study of crude drugs — dried or fresh natural products of plant, animal or mineral origin used as medicine or as raw material for medicine. Before you can use a crude drug you must be able to name it, place it in a system, and prove it is genuine. Those two jobs are classification and evaluation.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Crude drug: the natural material after collection and drying, before any chemical processing. Example: dried senna leaflets.`,`Biological source: the binomial (genus + species) of the plant or animal the drug comes from, plus the family.`,`Part used: leaf, bark, root, rhizome, seed, fruit, flower, whole herb, latex, gum.`,`Chief constituent: the chemical mainly responsible for activity, e.g. sennosides in senna.`,`Organised drug: has a cellular structure you can see under a microscope (leaf, bark, root).`,`Unorganised drug: no cellular structure — dried exudates, extracts, oils, gums, resins.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The five ways of classifying crude drugs`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`System`,`Basis`,`Example grouping`],rows:[[`Alphabetical`,`Name, A to Z`,`Acacia, Benzoin, Cinnamon (as in IP/BP)`],[`Taxonomic`,`Family, genus, species`,`All Solanaceae drugs together`],[`Morphological`,`Part used / external form`,`Leaves; barks; gums; latices`],[`Chemical`,`Chief constituent`,`Alkaloids; glycosides; volatile oils`],[`Pharmacological`,`Therapeutic action`,`Purgatives; cardiotonics; bitters`]],caption:`The five standard systems. GPAT usually asks which system groups unrelated plants together.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Most asked classification fact`,text:`Chemical classification is the system of choice for a phytochemist because it groups drugs by the constituent being isolated. Its drawback is that one drug with two constituent classes (e.g. cinchona has alkaloids and tannins) cannot be placed in one group.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Evaluation — proving a drug is genuine`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Organoleptic (morphological) — colour, odour, taste, size, shape, fracture, texture. The quickest and cheapest.`,`Microscopic — cell types, trichomes, stomata, crystals, starch, fibres. Essential for powdered drugs.`,`Physical — moisture, ash values, extractive values, swelling index, foaming index, melting point, optical rotation.`,`Chemical — colour tests, assays, acid value, saponification value, chromatography.`,`Biological — bioassay on living tissue or animals, used when chemistry cannot measure potency.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Order of evaluation`,text:`O-M-P-C-B: Organoleptic, Microscopic, Physical, Chemical, Biological — cheapest and fastest first, most expensive last.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Ash value tells you how much inorganic matter a drug carries. High acid-insoluble ash means sand and earth were not washed off. Extractive value tells you how much soluble matter a solvent can pull out, so a low alcohol-soluble extractive suggests the drug was already exhausted by someone else.`}]},{topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At exam level you need the exact definitions of each ash and extractive value, the quantitative microscopy constants and their formulae, and the stomatal and trichome types with named drug examples.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Ash values`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Value`,`How obtained`,`What it detects`],rows:[[`Total ash`,`Incinerate drug at 450 degrees C to constant weight`,`All inorganic matter: physiological + non-physiological`],[`Acid-insoluble ash`,`Boil total ash with dilute HCl, filter, ignite residue`,`Silica — sand and earthy adherent matter`],[`Water-soluble ash`,`Total ash minus residue left after washing with water`,`Water-soluble inorganics; detects exhausted drug`],[`Sulphated ash`,`Char with conc. sulphuric acid, then ignite`,`Non-volatile inorganic impurity in purified substances`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Acid-insoluble ash`,text:`Acid-insoluble ash is the single most useful ash value for detecting sand, soil and earthy adulteration, because silica does not dissolve in dilute hydrochloric acid. Total ash alone cannot separate physiological ash (from the plant) from non-physiological ash (added dirt).`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Extractive values and moisture`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Alcohol-soluble extractive: detects resins, tannins, glycosides; falls if the drug was exhausted with alcohol.`,`Water-soluble extractive: detects gums, mucilages, sugars, tannins, glycosides.`,`Ether-soluble extractive: detects fixed oils, volatile oils, waxes.`,`Moisture is determined by loss on drying (105 degrees C), by azeotropic distillation with toluene, or by Karl Fischer titration for thermolabile drugs.`,`Excess moisture invites enzymatic hydrolysis of glycosides and fungal growth — most crude drugs are kept below about 10 percent moisture.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Quantitative microscopy`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Constant`,`Definition`],rows:[[`Stomatal number`,`Average number of stomata per sq mm of epidermis`],[`Stomatal index`,`Percentage of stomata among total epidermal cells`],[`Vein-islet number`,`Number of vein-islets per sq mm midway between midrib and margin`],[`Veinlet termination number`,`Number of veinlet endings per sq mm of leaf`],[`Palisade ratio`,`Average number of palisade cells beneath one epidermal cell`],[`Lycopodium spore method`,`Counts particles against a known spore standard`]]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Stomatal index = [S / (E + S)] x 100`,caption:`S = number of stomata per unit area; E = number of ordinary epidermal cells in the same area. Unlike stomatal number, the index is nearly constant for a species and independent of leaf age and growing conditions.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Particles per mg = (N x 94,000 x W) / (S x M)`,caption:`N = particles of drug counted; S = lycopodium spores counted in the same fields; W = weight in mg of lycopodium taken; M = weight in mg of sample taken. 94,000 is the number of lycopodium spores per mg of standard lycopodium.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Stomata type`,`Other name`,`Drug example`],rows:[[`Anomocytic`,`Ranunculaceous, irregular-celled`,`Digitalis, Vasaka`],[`Anisocytic`,`Cruciferous, unequal-celled`,`Belladonna, Datura, Stramonium`],[`Diacytic`,`Caryophyllaceous, cross-celled`,`Peppermint, Vasaka (also)`],[`Paracytic`,`Rubiaceous, parallel-celled`,`Senna, Coca`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Stomatal number vs stomatal index`,text:`Stomatal number changes with leaf age, humidity and light, so it is a poor identity test. Stomatal index is a ratio, so it stays constant for a species — that is why the pharmacopoeia uses the index for evaluation.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Swelling index: volume in mL taken up by swelling of 1 g of plant material under stated conditions. Ispaghula husk has a swelling index of not less than 40 — the highest among official drugs.`,`Foaming index: measures saponins. It is the reciprocal relation 1000 / a, where a is the volume in mL of the decoction in the tube showing 1 cm of persistent foam.`,`Bitterness value: expressed in units equivalent to the bitterness of a solution containing 1 g of quinine hydrochloride in 2000 mL of water.`,`Haemolytic index: potency of a saponin drug relative to standard saponin (Saponinum album).`,`Volatile oil content is determined by hydrodistillation in a Clevenger apparatus.`]}]},{topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The high-yield end of this topic is the numbers: reported leaf constants for the two senna species, the reasoning behind chemotaxonomy, and biological assays that chemistry cannot replace.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Constant`,`Cassia angustifolia (Tinnevelly)`,`Cassia acutifolia (Alexandrian)`],rows:[[`Vein-islet number`,`About 19.5 to 22.5`,`About 25.0 to 29.5`],[`Origin`,`Tinnevelly, South India`,`Sudan, Nile valley`],[`Leaflet shape`,`Larger, lanceolate, less asymmetric`,`Smaller, broader, more asymmetric`]],caption:`Vein-islet number is the classic leaf constant used to separate the two official sennas.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Vein-islet number`,text:`Vein-islet number was introduced by Levin. It is constant for a species and unaffected by leaf age, so it distinguishes closely related leaf drugs — most famously the two sennas, and belladonna from its substitutes.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Modern extensions of classification`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Chemotaxonomic classification: uses the distribution of chemical markers to support taxonomy, e.g. betalains occur only in Caryophyllales, which excludes those families from producing anthocyanins.`,`Serotaxonomic classification: uses immunological cross-reactivity of plant proteins to judge relatedness.`,`Numerical or phenetic taxonomy: computes similarity coefficients from many characters, giving equal weight to each.`,`DNA-based classification: barcoding loci rbcL, matK, ITS2 and psbA-trnH identify a species independent of plant part or age.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Biological evaluation`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug / substance`,`Biological method`],rows:[[`Digitalis`,`Pigeon emetic method; also cat and guinea-pig methods`],[`Insulin`,`Rabbit blood sugar method / mouse convulsion method`],[`Vitamin D`,`Rat line test (healing of rachitic metaphysis)`],[`Antibiotics`,`Cup-plate and cylinder-plate diffusion assay`],[`Saponins`,`Haemolysis of erythrocyte suspension`],[`Ergot / oxytocics`,`Rat uterus or cock comb method`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Why bioassay survives`,text:`Bioassay is used when the active principle is unknown, a mixture, or chemically unstable — not because it is more accurate. Chemical assay is always preferred when a specific marker can be measured, because bioassay has far wider confidence limits.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Foreign organic matter in most pharmacopoeial crude drugs is limited to not more than 2 percent w/w.`,`Loss on drying is expressed as percentage w/w and, unless a monograph says otherwise, is performed at 105 degrees C to constant weight.`,`Karl Fischer titration measures water specifically (iodine, sulphur dioxide, a base and methanol), so it is used when a drug contains other volatile constituents such as volatile oil.`,`Azeotropic (toluene) distillation is preferred for drugs rich in volatile oils, because loss on drying would over-read the water content.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Which ash for which fault`,text:`Sand and soil — acid-insoluble ash. Exhausted or previously extracted drug — water-soluble ash and low extractive values. Overall inorganic load — total ash.`}]},{topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This topic covers the non-nitrogenous bulk constituents of plants and animals — starches, gums, mucilages, marine polysaccharides, fixed oils, fats and waxes. Most are used as excipients, laxatives or nutritional agents rather than as potent drugs.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Gum: a pathological exudate formed when a plant is injured; it dissolves or swells in water.`,`Mucilage: a normal physiological product of the plant, found inside cells or ducts.`,`Fixed oil: an ester of glycerol with long-chain fatty acids, liquid at room temperature, leaves a permanent grease spot on paper.`,`Fat: the same chemistry as a fixed oil but solid at room temperature.`,`Wax: an ester of a long-chain fatty acid with a long-chain monohydric alcohol, not with glycerol.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Biological source`,`Family`,`Part / nature`],rows:[[`Starch`,`Zea mays, Oryza sativa, Triticum aestivum, Solanum tuberosum`,`Poaceae; Solanaceae`,`Grain / tuber`],[`Acacia`,`Acacia senegal`,`Leguminosae`,`Dried gummy exudate of stem and branches`],[`Tragacanth`,`Astragalus gummifer`,`Leguminosae`,`Dried gummy exudate`],[`Agar`,`Gelidium and Gracilaria species`,`Gelidiaceae (Rhodophyceae)`,`Dried mucilage of red algae`],[`Alginate`,`Laminaria and Macrocystis species`,`Phaeophyceae`,`Extract of brown seaweed`],[`Guar gum`,`Cyamopsis tetragonoloba`,`Leguminosae`,`Endosperm of seed`],[`Isabgol`,`Plantago ovata`,`Plantaginaceae`,`Husk of seed`],[`Honey`,`Apis mellifera`,`Apidae`,`Saccharine secretion deposited in the comb`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Solubility separates the two big gums`,text:`Acacia dissolves completely in water to give a clear mucilage. Tragacanth does not dissolve — it swells into a stiff gel. That is why acacia is the emulsifying agent and tragacanth the suspending and thickening agent.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Starch is a mixture of amylose, a straight chain of glucose joined by alpha-1,4 links, and amylopectin, a branched chain that also has alpha-1,6 links at the branch points. Iodine solution gives a blue colour with starch because iodine slots into the amylose helix.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Fixed oil`,`Biological source`,`Family`,`Chief use`],rows:[[`Castor oil`,`Ricinus communis`,`Euphorbiaceae`,`Purgative, ophthalmic emollient`],[`Arachis oil`,`Arachis hypogaea`,`Leguminosae`,`Solvent for oily injections, edible`],[`Olive oil`,`Olea europaea`,`Oleaceae`,`Emollient, laxative, edible`],[`Cod liver oil`,`Gadus morrhua and other Gadus species`,`Gadidae`,`Source of vitamins A and D`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Oil vs volatile oil on paper`,text:`Drop both on filter paper and warm. The fixed oil leaves a permanent translucent stain; the volatile oil evaporates and the stain disappears.`}]},{topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Exam questions here concentrate on chief constituents, the identifying chemical tests, starch granule morphology, and the analytical constants of fixed oils.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Starch granule identification`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Starch`,`Granule shape and size`,`Hilum`],rows:[[`Maize`,`Polygonal and rounded, 10 to 35 micrometre`,`Central, cleft or stellate`],[`Rice`,`Very small polygonal, 2 to 10 micrometre, in compound masses`,`Central, indistinct`],[`Wheat`,`Two sizes: large lenticular and small spherical`,`Central, indistinct`],[`Potato`,`Largest, ovoid, up to about 100 micrometre`,`Eccentric with marked concentric striations`]],caption:`Potato starch is the largest and the only one with a strongly eccentric hilum — the classic identification question.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Gums, mucilages and their tests`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Chief constituent`,`Identifying test`],rows:[[`Acacia`,`Arabin (Ca, Mg, K salts of arabic acid)`,`Lead subacetate gives a heavy white precipitate`],[`Tragacanth`,`Bassorin 60-70 pc, tragacanthin about 30 pc`,`Swells but does not dissolve; yellow with alkali`],[`Karaya (Indian tragacanth)`,`Acetylated polysaccharide`,`Smells of acetic acid; stains red with ruthenium red`],[`Agar`,`Agarose and agaropectin`,`Gels at about 1.5 pc; sets near 35-40 deg C, melts near 85 deg C`],[`Sodium alginate`,`Salt of alginic acid (mannuronic + guluronic acid)`,`Calcium chloride gives a gel; acid gives alginic acid`],[`Guar gum`,`Galactomannan, mannose : galactose about 2 : 1`,`Very high viscosity in cold water`],[`Honey`,`Fructose about 38 pc, glucose about 31 pc`,`Fiehe test — red colour means added invert sugar`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Acacia and hydrogen peroxide`,text:`Acacia contains an oxidase (peroxidase) enzyme, so it spoils preparations containing easily oxidised drugs and gives a false peroxidase reaction. Heating the mucilage to 100 degrees C for a short time destroys the enzyme without harming the gum.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Fiehe test`,text:`Fiehe test uses resorcinol in hydrochloric acid. A cherry-red colour indicates hydroxymethylfurfural, which comes from commercial invert sugar or overheated honey — it is a test for adulteration, not for honey itself.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Analytical constants of fixed oils`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Constant`,`Definition`,`What it measures`],rows:[[`Acid value`,`mg KOH to neutralise free acids in 1 g of oil`,`Rancidity / free fatty acid`],[`Saponification value`,`mg KOH to neutralise free acids and saponify esters in 1 g`,`Inversely proportional to fatty acid chain length`],[`Iodine value`,`g of iodine absorbed by 100 g of oil`,`Degree of unsaturation`],[`Acetyl value`,`mg KOH to neutralise acetic acid from 1 g of acetylated oil`,`Free hydroxyl groups`],[`Ester value`,`Saponification value minus acid value`,`Esterified acid content`],[`Reichert-Meissl value`,`mL of 0.1 N alkali for volatile soluble fatty acids from 5 g`,`Butyric-type acids, used for butter/ghee`],[`Polenske value`,`mL of 0.1 N alkali for volatile insoluble fatty acids`,`Coconut oil adulteration of ghee`]]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Ester value = Saponification value - Acid value`,caption:`All three are expressed as mg of potassium hydroxide per gram of oil.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Two castor oil facts`,text:`Castor oil is the only common fixed oil that is soluble in an equal volume of alcohol, because ricinoleic acid carries a hydroxyl group. The same hydroxyl gives it an unusually high acetyl value of roughly 144 to 150.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Wax`,`Source`,`Chief ester`],rows:[[`Beeswax`,`Apis mellifera comb`,`Myricyl palmitate`],[`Carnauba wax`,`Copernicia cerifera leaves (Palmae)`,`Myricyl cerotate`],[`Spermaceti`,`Physeter macrocephalus`,`Cetyl palmitate`],[`Wool fat (lanolin)`,`Wool of Ovis aries`,`Cholesteryl esters`],[`Jojoba`,`Simmondsia chinensis seed`,`Liquid wax esters`]]}]},{topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced material is the specific colour tests used to detect one oil in another, the toxic principles left behind during oil expression, and the marine polysaccharide chemistry.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Test`,`Reagent`,`Detects`],rows:[[`Baudouin test`,`Furfural in alcohol + conc. HCl, crimson colour`,`Sesame (til) oil, due to sesamolin`],[`Bellier test`,`Alcoholic KOH + acetic acid, turbidity`,`Arachis oil, due to arachidic acid`],[`Halphen test`,`Carbon disulphide + sulphur, red colour`,`Cottonseed oil`],[`Hexabromide test`,`Bromination, insoluble bromide`,`Linseed oil (highly unsaturated)`],[`Fiehe test`,`Resorcinol in HCl, red colour`,`Invert sugar in honey`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Ricin and ricinine`,text:`Castor seed contains ricin, a highly toxic toxalbumin, and ricinine, an alkaloid. Neither is soluble in the oil, so both remain in the seed cake after cold expression — that is why the oil is safe and the cake is not fed to animals.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Oil`,`Marker fatty acid`,`Approximate share`],rows:[[`Castor`,`Ricinoleic acid`,`About 85 to 90 pc`],[`Olive`,`Oleic acid`,`About 56 to 85 pc`],[`Linseed`,`Linolenic acid`,`High; drying oil, iodine value about 170-200`],[`Chaulmoogra`,`Chaulmoogric and hydnocarpic acid`,`Cyclopentenyl acids, used in leprosy`],[`Coconut`,`Lauric acid`,`Very high saponification value, about 250-264`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Saponification value and molecular weight`,text:`A high saponification value means SHORT chain fatty acids, because more ester groups exist per gram. Coconut oil, rich in lauric acid, has a high value; linseed and castor, with long chains, have lower values. Students routinely invert this relationship.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Marine and seed polysaccharides`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Agar is a galactan: agarose is the neutral gelling fraction, agaropectin the sulphated non-gelling fraction. Agar is not digested by most bacteria, which is why it is the standard microbiological culture medium.`,`Alginic acid is a linear copolymer of beta-D-mannuronic acid and alpha-L-guluronic acid; guluronic-rich blocks form the egg-box gel with calcium ions.`,`Calcium alginate is used as an absorbable haemostatic dressing.`,`Carrageenan comes from Chondrus crispus (Irish moss); the kappa and iota forms gel, the lambda form only thickens.`,`Isabgol husk is a bulk-forming laxative; the swelling index of the husk is not less than 40, far above whole seed.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Algal colour to phylum`,text:`Agar and carrageenan are RED algae products (Rhodophyceae); alginates are BROWN algae products (Phaeophyceae). Red for the gel you culture on, brown for the salt you thicken with.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Cod liver oil is obtained from the fresh livers of Gadus species and is standardised for vitamins A and D. Shark liver oil is the richest natural source of squalene, and halibut liver oil from Hippoglossus species is far more potent in vitamin A per gram than cod liver oil, so it is given in much smaller doses.`}]},{topicId:`pg-glycosides`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A glycoside is a compound in which a sugar is joined to a non-sugar part through an acetal linkage. On hydrolysis it splits into the sugar (glycone) and the non-sugar (aglycone or genin). Almost all of the pharmacological activity sits in the aglycone; the sugar mainly controls solubility, absorption and the ease with which the molecule reaches its target.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Glycone: the sugar portion — glucose, rhamnose, and in cardiac glycosides special deoxy sugars such as digitoxose and cymarose.`,`Aglycone or genin: the active non-sugar portion — a steroid, an anthraquinone, a flavone, and so on.`,`Glycosidic linkage: the bond between the anomeric carbon of the sugar and the aglycone. It is hydrolysed by acid and by specific enzymes.`,`Glycosides themselves are usually colourless, bitter, water-soluble solids that are optically active and levorotatory.`,`They are neutral, so they are NOT precipitated by alkaloidal reagents — a favourite exam contrast with alkaloids.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Two ways of classifying glycosides`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`By linkage atom`,`Bond`,`Example`],rows:[[`O-glycoside`,`Sugar joined through oxygen`,`Digitoxin, sennosides, salicin`],[`C-glycoside`,`Sugar joined through carbon, acid resistant`,`Aloin, barbaloin, cascarosides`],[`N-glycoside`,`Sugar joined through nitrogen`,`Nucleosides such as adenosine`],[`S-glycoside`,`Sugar joined through sulphur (thioglycoside)`,`Sinigrin of black mustard`]],caption:`C-glycosides resist ordinary acid hydrolysis, which is exactly why aloe needs the modified Borntrager test.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Chemical class`,`Aglycone type`,`Drug example`],rows:[[`Cardiac (cardenolide/bufadienolide)`,`Steroid + lactone`,`Digitalis, strophanthus, squill`],[`Anthraquinone`,`Anthracene derivative`,`Senna, aloe, rhubarb, cascara`],[`Saponin`,`Steroid or triterpene`,`Liquorice, dioscorea, ginseng`],[`Cyanogenetic`,`Cyanohydrin`,`Wild cherry, bitter almond`],[`Isothiocyanate`,`Mustard oil precursor`,`Black and white mustard`],[`Flavonoid`,`Flavone / flavonol`,`Rutin, hesperidin`],[`Coumarin`,`Benzo-alpha-pyrone`,`Ammi majus, psoralea`],[`Bitter`,`Varied, intensely bitter`,`Gentian, quassia, picrorhiza`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Aglycone carries the activity`,text:`Removing the sugar rarely abolishes activity but it changes potency and pharmacokinetics. In cardiac glycosides the sugar increases water solubility and prolongs the duration of action; the steroid-lactone genin alone is far less useful clinically.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Linkage letters`,text:`O-C-N-S: Ordinary (most drugs), Carbon (aloe), Nitrogen (nucleosides), Sulphur (mustard).`},{kind:`para`,text:`Because glycosides are hydrolysed by enzymes present in the same plant, crude drugs rich in glycosides are dried quickly at a moderate temperature. Slow drying in a damp atmosphere lets the enzyme act and the glycoside content falls — this is why senna and digitalis leaves are dried rapidly.`}]},{topicId:`pg-glycosides`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable core of this topic is a set of named colour tests and exactly which part of the molecule each one detects, together with a source table you can recall on sight.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Cardiac glycosides`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Biological source`,`Family`,`Part used`,`Chief constituent`,`Use`],rows:[[`Digitalis (purple foxglove)`,`Digitalis purpurea`,`Scrophulariaceae`,`Dried leaf`,`Purpurea glycosides A and B, digitoxin, gitoxin`,`Congestive heart failure`],[`Digitalis lanata`,`Digitalis lanata`,`Scrophulariaceae`,`Dried leaf`,`Lanatosides A, B, C; source of digoxin`,`Digoxin manufacture`],[`Strophanthus`,`Strophanthus kombe, S. gratus`,`Apocynaceae`,`Ripe dried seed`,`K-strophanthoside; ouabain (G-strophanthin)`,`Rapid-acting cardiotonic`],[`Squill`,`Urginea indica, Drimia maritima`,`Liliaceae`,`Dried bulb slices`,`Scillaren A and B (bufadienolides)`,`Cardiotonic, expectorant, rat poison`],[`Thevetia`,`Thevetia neriifolia`,`Apocynaceae`,`Seed`,`Thevetin, peruvoside`,`Cardiotonic (toxic)`]]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Test`,`Reagent`,`What it detects`,`Positive result`],rows:[[`Keller-Kiliani`,`Glacial acetic acid + trace FeCl3, layered on conc. H2SO4`,`The 2-deoxy sugar`,`Reddish-brown ring at junction, blue acetic acid layer`],[`Baljet`,`Sodium picrate (picric acid + NaOH)`,`The unsaturated lactone ring`,`Orange to orange-red colour`],[`Legal`,`Sodium nitroprusside in pyridine + NaOH`,`Five-membered (alpha,beta-unsaturated) lactone`,`Deep red or pink colour`],[`Kedde`,`3,5-dinitrobenzoic acid + alkali`,`Five-membered lactone (cardenolide)`,`Purple to blue colour`],[`Raymond`,`m-dinitrobenzene + alcoholic KOH`,`Active methylene / lactone`,`Violet colour`],[`Liebermann-Burchard`,`Acetic anhydride + conc. H2SO4`,`Steroid nucleus`,`Blue-green colour`]],caption:`Split them by target: deoxy sugar = Keller-Kiliani; lactone = Baljet, Legal, Kedde, Raymond; steroid ring = Liebermann-Burchard.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Keller-Kiliani is the deoxy-sugar test`,text:`Keller-Kiliani is positive for glycosides carrying a 2-deoxy sugar (digitoxose, cymarose). Ouabain gives a poor Keller-Kiliani response because its sugar is rhamnose, not a 2-deoxy sugar — the classic exception.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Anthraquinone glycosides`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Biological source`,`Family`,`Part used`,`Chief constituent`],rows:[[`Senna`,`Cassia angustifolia, C. acutifolia`,`Leguminosae (Caesalpiniaceae)`,`Dried leaflet and pod`,`Sennosides A, B, C, D (dianthrone)`],[`Aloe`,`Aloe barbadensis, A. ferox, A. perryi`,`Liliaceae`,`Dried latex of leaf`,`Barbaloin (aloin), a C-glycoside`],[`Rhubarb`,`Rheum palmatum, R. emodi`,`Polygonaceae`,`Dried rhizome and root`,`Emodin, chrysophanol, rhein; also tannins`],[`Cascara`,`Rhamnus purshiana`,`Rhamnaceae`,`Dried bark, aged 1 year`,`Cascarosides A to D (C-glycosides)`],[`Frangula`,`Rhamnus frangula`,`Rhamnaceae`,`Dried bark`,`Glucofrangulins`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Borntrager test: extract the drug with an immiscible organic solvent, shake the organic layer with dilute ammonia — a pink to red ammoniacal layer means free anthraquinone.`,`Modified Borntrager test: boil the drug first with ferric chloride and dilute hydrochloric acid. This oxidises anthranol and anthrone forms to anthraquinone and cleaves C-glycosides, so aloe and cascara now give the colour.`,`Anthraquinone laxatives act on the colon after bacterial hydrolysis, so the onset is 6 to 12 hours.`,`Aloe is evaluated for aloin content; the drug is the dried latex, not the mucilaginous gel used in cosmetics.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Why aloe needs modified Borntrager`,text:`Barbaloin is a C-glycoside — the sugar is attached by a carbon-carbon bond, which ordinary acid hydrolysis cannot break, and it exists in the reduced anthrone form. Ferric chloride supplies the oxidation, so only the modified test gives a pink ammoniacal layer.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Saponins and the remaining classes`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Biological source`,`Family`,`Chief constituent`,`Type`],rows:[[`Liquorice`,`Glycyrrhiza glabra`,`Leguminosae`,`Glycyrrhizin (glycyrrhizinic acid)`,`Triterpenoid saponin`],[`Dioscorea`,`Dioscorea deltoidea, D. floribunda`,`Dioscoreaceae`,`Diosgenin (from dioscin)`,`Steroidal saponin`],[`Ginseng`,`Panax ginseng`,`Araliaceae`,`Ginsenosides (panaxosides)`,`Triterpenoid saponin`],[`Sarsaparilla`,`Smilax species`,`Liliaceae`,`Sarsasapogenin, smilagenin`,`Steroidal saponin`],[`Wild cherry`,`Prunus serotina`,`Rosaceae`,`Prunasin, enzyme prunase`,`Cyanogenetic`],[`Bitter almond`,`Prunus amygdalus var. amara`,`Rosaceae`,`Amygdalin, enzyme emulsin`,`Cyanogenetic`],[`Black mustard`,`Brassica nigra`,`Cruciferae`,`Sinigrin, enzyme myrosin`,`Isothiocyanate (S-glycoside)`],[`White mustard`,`Brassica alba`,`Cruciferae`,`Sinalbin`,`Isothiocyanate`],[`Rutin`,`Eucalyptus macrorhyncha, Fagopyrum esculentum`,`Myrtaceae; Polygonaceae`,`Rutin (quercetin-3-rutinoside)`,`Flavonoid`],[`Gentian`,`Gentiana lutea`,`Gentianaceae`,`Gentiopicrin, amarogentin`,`Bitter`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Foam test: shake an aqueous extract vigorously; a honeycomb froth persisting for over 15 minutes indicates saponins.`,`Haemolysis test: saponins complex with cholesterol in the erythrocyte membrane and lyse red cells, giving a clear red solution. Potency is expressed as the haemolytic index.`,`Grignard test and the sodium picrate paper test detect hydrocyanic acid released from cyanogenetic glycosides (paper turns brick red).`,`Shinoda (cyanidin) test: magnesium turnings plus concentrated HCl give a pink to magenta colour with flavonoids.`,`Coumarins fluoresce blue or green in UV light, especially after treatment with alkali.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Saponin one-liners`,text:`Saponins foam like soap, haemolyse red blood cells, and are highly toxic if injected but relatively safe orally because they are poorly absorbed. Fish poisoning by saponin-containing plants is a standard MCQ.`}]},{topicId:`pg-glycosides`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions turn on structure-activity relationships of the cardenolide skeleton, the enzymatic conversions that occur during drying, and the specific sugars that identify each glycoside.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Cardenolides carry a five-membered alpha,beta-unsaturated butenolide ring at C-17 and are 23-carbon steroids: digitoxin, digoxin, ouabain.`,`Bufadienolides carry a six-membered alpha-pyrone ring at C-17 and are 24-carbon: scillaren A of squill, bufotoxin of toad venom, hellebrin.`,`The A/B ring junction is cis and the C/D junction is also cis — this unusual cis-trans-cis geometry gives the U-shaped molecule needed for Na+/K+-ATPase binding.`,`A beta-oriented hydroxyl at C-14 is essential for activity; epimerisation or dehydration at C-14 abolishes it.`,`Adding hydroxyls increases polarity: digitoxin (no C-12 OH) is the most lipophilic and most protein bound, digoxin has a C-12 OH, and ouabain is the most polar with several hydroxyls.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Glycoside`,`Characteristic sugar`,`Note`],rows:[[`Digitoxin`,`Three digitoxose units`,`2-deoxy sugar, strong Keller-Kiliani`],[`Digoxin`,`Three digitoxose units`,`Differs from digitoxin by a 12-beta-OH`],[`Lanatoside C`,`Digitoxose x3 + acetyl + glucose`,`Deacetylation and deglucosidation give digoxin`],[`K-strophanthoside`,`Cymarose + 2 glucose`,`Cymarose is a 2-deoxy sugar`],[`Ouabain`,`Rhamnose only`,`Not a 2-deoxy sugar, weak Keller-Kiliani`],[`Sennoside A and B`,`Two glucose units`,`A and B are optical isomers (A is dextro, B is meso)`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Primary versus secondary glycosides`,text:`The glycoside as it exists in the living plant is the primary glycoside (purpurea glycoside A, sennoside A, lanatoside C). Partial enzymatic loss of one sugar during drying gives the secondary glycoside (digitoxin, rhein anthrone glycosides, acetyldigoxin). Digitoxin is therefore a secondary, not a primary, glycoside of Digitalis purpurea.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Purpurea glycoside A - (glucose, by digipurpidase) = Digitoxin`,caption:`Digipurpidase is the endogenous enzyme of Digitalis purpurea; the same enzyme converts purpurea glycoside B to gitoxin. Rapid drying at 60 degrees C inactivates it and preserves the primary glycosides.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Fine points that decide marks`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Amygdalin is hydrolysed by emulsin to give benzaldehyde, glucose and hydrocyanic acid — the bitter almond odour is benzaldehyde.`,`Sinigrin is hydrolysed by myrosin (myrosinase) to allyl isothiocyanate, the vesicant of black mustard; white mustard gives the non-volatile p-hydroxybenzyl isothiocyanate, which is why black mustard is the rubefacient.`,`Glycyrrhizin is about 50 times sweeter than sucrose; excess causes pseudo-aldosteronism with sodium retention, oedema and hypokalaemia because glycyrrhetinic acid inhibits 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase.`,`Diosgenin from Dioscorea is the classical starting material for the Marker degradation used to make corticosteroids and sex hormones.`,`Rutin and hesperidin are flavonoid glycosides used to reduce capillary fragility; rutin is quercetin plus rutinose (rhamnose + glucose).`,`Aloe-emodin, rhein, chrysophanol, emodin and physcion are the five common anthraquinone aglycones; rhein is a carboxylic acid, chrysophanol has neither carboxyl nor extra hydroxyl.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Anthracene oxidation states`,text:`Anthraquinone is the oxidised form; anthrone and anthranol are the reduced forms; dianthrone is a dimer of two anthrone units. Sennosides are dianthrone glycosides — this is why fresh senna causes griping and stored, partly oxidised senna is milder.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Lactone ring size`,text:`CARDenolide = 5-membered ring, C23. BUFadienolide = 6-membered ring, C24. Bufa is bigger in both ring and carbon count.`}]},{topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Alkaloids are basic, nitrogen-containing compounds of plant origin, usually with the nitrogen inside a heterocyclic ring, and almost always pharmacologically active in small doses. They are the largest and most potent group of plant constituents — morphine, atropine, quinine, reserpine and caffeine are all alkaloids.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Basic: they react with acids to form salts. The free base dissolves in organic solvents; the salt dissolves in water.`,`Most are bitter, colourless, crystalline, non-volatile solids that are optically active and levorotatory.`,`Exceptions worth remembering: nicotine, coniine and sparteine are liquids; berberine and colchicine are coloured (yellow); caffeine, colchicine and piperine are non-basic or very weakly basic.`,`Names end in -ine. The plant stores them as salts of organic acids such as meconic, quinic, malic and tartaric acid.`,`Pseudoalkaloids are not derived from amino acids (caffeine from purines, solanine and conessine from steroids); protoalkaloids have nitrogen outside the ring (ephedrine, mescaline, colchicine).`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`General tests — alkaloidal precipitating reagents`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Reagent`,`Composition`,`Precipitate colour`],rows:[[`Mayer`,`Potassium mercuric iodide`,`Cream or white`],[`Dragendorff`,`Potassium bismuth iodide`,`Orange to reddish-brown`],[`Wagner`,`Iodine in potassium iodide`,`Reddish-brown`],[`Hager`,`Saturated picric acid solution`,`Yellow`],[`Tannic acid`,`Tannic acid solution`,`Buff / precipitate`],[`Marme`,`Potassium cadmium iodide`,`White`]],caption:`These are general, not specific — they tell you an alkaloid is present, not which one.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Reagent metals`,text:`Mayer = Mercury, Dragendorff = Bismuth (D for the deep orange), Wagner = iodine, Hager = picric acid (H for the yellow "Hager gold"). Marme = Cadmium.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Extraction depends on the acid-base behaviour. In the Stas-Otto approach the powdered drug is moistened with an alkali such as ammonia or lime to liberate the free bases, then the free bases are extracted with an organic solvent like chloroform, and finally shaken with dilute acid so the alkaloids move back into the aqueous layer as salts, leaving fats and pigments behind in the solvent.`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Powder and defat the drug if it is oily.`,`Basify with ammonia, lime or sodium carbonate to convert salts into free bases.`,`Extract the free bases with chloroform, ether or dichloromethane.`,`Shake the organic extract with dilute acid — alkaloids pass into water as salts.`,`Re-basify the aqueous acid layer and re-extract with organic solvent to obtain purified total alkaloids.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The one drug with an inorganic acid`,text:`Opium alkaloids exist mainly as salts of meconic acid. Meconic acid gives a deep red colour with ferric chloride that is not discharged by hydrochloric acid — the classic forensic test for opium.`}]},{topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Nearly every alkaloid MCQ reduces to one of three questions: which nucleus, which biological source, or which chemical test. Learn the nucleus table and the source table together.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Classification by nucleus`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Nucleus`,`Alkaloid examples`,`Drug`],rows:[[`Pyridine-piperidine`,`Nicotine, lobeline, piperine, coniine, arecoline`,`Tobacco, lobelia, pepper, hemlock, areca`],[`Tropane`,`Atropine, hyoscyamine, hyoscine, cocaine`,`Belladonna, datura, hyoscyamus, coca`],[`Quinoline`,`Quinine, quinidine, cinchonine, cinchonidine`,`Cinchona`],[`Isoquinoline`,`Morphine, codeine, papaverine, emetine, tubocurarine, berberine`,`Opium, ipecac, curare, berberis`],[`Indole`,`Reserpine, ergotamine, vincristine, strychnine, physostigmine`,`Rauwolfia, ergot, vinca, nux vomica, calabar bean`],[`Imidazole`,`Pilocarpine`,`Jaborandi (Pilocarpus)`],[`Purine`,`Caffeine, theophylline, theobromine`,`Tea, coffee, cocoa`],[`Steroidal`,`Solanine, conessine, protoveratrine`,`Solanum, kurchi, veratrum`],[`Alkaloidal amine (protoalkaloid)`,`Ephedrine, mescaline, colchicine`,`Ephedra, peyote, colchicum`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Source table`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Biological source`,`Family`,`Part used`,`Chief constituent`,`Use`],rows:[[`Opium`,`Papaver somniferum`,`Papaveraceae`,`Dried latex of unripe capsule`,`Morphine 10-16 pc, codeine, papaverine, thebaine, narcotine`,`Analgesic, antitussive, antispasmodic`],[`Belladonna`,`Atropa belladonna`,`Solanaceae`,`Dried leaf and root`,`Hyoscyamine, atropine`,`Antispasmodic, mydriatic`],[`Datura (stramonium)`,`Datura stramonium, D. metel`,`Solanaceae`,`Dried leaf and seed`,`Hyoscyamine, hyoscine`,`Antiasthmatic, antispasmodic`],[`Hyoscyamus`,`Hyoscyamus niger`,`Solanaceae`,`Dried leaf and top`,`Hyoscyamine, hyoscine`,`Sedative antispasmodic`],[`Cinchona`,`Cinchona ledgeriana, C. succirubra`,`Rubiaceae`,`Dried bark`,`Quinine, quinidine, cinchonine, cinchonidine`,`Antimalarial, antiarrhythmic`],[`Ipecacuanha`,`Cephaelis ipecacuanha`,`Rubiaceae`,`Dried root and rhizome`,`Emetine, cephaeline`,`Emetic, amoebicide`],[`Ergot`,`Claviceps purpurea on Secale cereale`,`Clavicipitaceae (fungus)`,`Dried sclerotium`,`Ergotamine, ergometrine, ergotoxine`,`Oxytocic, migraine`],[`Rauwolfia`,`Rauwolfia serpentina`,`Apocynaceae`,`Dried root`,`Reserpine, rescinnamine, ajmaline`,`Antihypertensive, tranquilliser`],[`Vinca`,`Catharanthus roseus`,`Apocynaceae`,`Whole plant, leaf`,`Vincristine, vinblastine`,`Anticancer`],[`Nux vomica`,`Strychnos nux-vomica`,`Loganiaceae`,`Dried ripe seed`,`Strychnine, brucine`,`Bitter tonic, CNS stimulant (toxic)`],[`Ephedra`,`Ephedra gerardiana, E. sinica`,`Ephedraceae`,`Dried stem`,`Ephedrine, pseudoephedrine`,`Bronchodilator, nasal decongestant`],[`Coca`,`Erythroxylum coca`,`Erythroxylaceae`,`Dried leaf`,`Cocaine`,`Local anaesthetic`],[`Tea`,`Camellia sinensis`,`Theaceae`,`Dried leaf`,`Caffeine 1-4 pc, tannins`,`CNS stimulant`],[`Coffee`,`Coffea arabica`,`Rubiaceae`,`Dried ripe seed`,`Caffeine 1-2 pc, chlorogenic acid`,`CNS stimulant, diuretic`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Specific identification tests`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Test`,`Reagent / observation`,`Detects`],rows:[[`Vitali-Morin`,`Fuming nitric acid, evaporate, then alcoholic KOH — violet`,`Tropane alkaloids (atropine, hyoscine)`],[`Murexide (Weidel)`,`Chlorine water or H2O2 + HCl, then ammonia — purple`,`Purine alkaloids (caffeine, theobromine)`],[`Thalleioquin`,`Bromine water then ammonia — emerald green`,`Quinine`],[`Meconic acid test`,`Ferric chloride — deep red, not discharged by HCl`,`Opium`],[`Van Urk`,`p-DMAB (Ehrlich reagent) in acid — blue`,`Ergot / indole alkaloids`],[`Nux vomica test`,`Conc. HNO3 on the endosperm — yellow-orange (brucine)`,`Brucine in nux vomica`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Thalleioquin is quinine only`,text:`Quinine gives an emerald-green thalleioquin colour with bromine water and ammonia because it carries a 6-methoxy group on the quinoline ring. Cinchonine and cinchonidine lack the methoxy group and give no thalleioquin — a routine way to distinguish them.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Atropine versus hyoscyamine`,text:`The living plant contains l-hyoscyamine. Atropine is the racemic dl-hyoscyamine formed by racemisation during extraction and drying, and it is about half as active as the natural l-isomer. So the alkaloid of belladonna in the field is hyoscyamine, not atropine.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Opium is the air-dried milky exudate obtained by incising unripe capsules; official opium contains not less than 9.5 percent anhydrous morphine.`,`Ergot is the dried sclerotium of the fungus growing on rye; the toxic condition it causes is ergotism, historically called St. Anthony fire.`,`Ipecac is evaluated on total alkaloids: Cartagena (Cephaelis acuminata) has a higher cephaeline to emetine ratio than Rio ipecac.`,`Rauwolfia is the classic example of an indole alkaloid drug that depletes stored catecholamines and serotonin.`,`Cocaine is a tropane ester of benzoic acid and methyl alcohol with ecgonine; atropine is a tropane ester of tropic acid with tropine.`]}]},{topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced tier is biosynthetic precursors, separations that rely on subtle chemical differences, and the exceptions that examiners use to catch rote learning.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Amino acid precursor`,`Alkaloid class`,`Examples`],rows:[[`Ornithine`,`Tropane, pyrrolidine, pyrrolizidine`,`Atropine, hyoscine, nicotine (pyrrolidine ring)`],[`Lysine`,`Piperidine, quinolizidine`,`Lobeline, sparteine, coniine (partly polyketide)`],[`Phenylalanine / tyrosine`,`Isoquinoline, phenylethylamine`,`Morphine, papaverine, emetine, ephedrine`],[`Tryptophan`,`Indole, quinoline (via strictosidine)`,`Reserpine, strychnine, quinine, ergot alkaloids`],[`Histidine`,`Imidazole`,`Pilocarpine`],[`Anthranilic acid`,`Quinazoline, acridine`,`Vasicine (peganine), febrifugine`]],caption:`Quinine is biosynthetically an INDOLE-derived alkaloid that is rearranged to a quinoline nucleus — a favourite catch question.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Structure class is not biosynthetic class`,text:`Quinine is classified structurally as a quinoline alkaloid but arises from tryptophan through strictosidine. Similarly caffeine is a purine pseudoalkaloid not derived from an amino acid, and ephedrine is a protoalkaloid with the nitrogen outside any ring.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Separation of quinine from quinidine uses the differing solubility of their tartrates and sulphates; quinine sulphate is far less soluble in water than quinidine sulphate, and quinine sulphate fluoresces blue in dilute sulphuric acid.`,`Morphine is separated from the other opium alkaloids because it is phenolic and therefore soluble in alkali — the classic phenolic versus non-phenolic split. Narcotine (noscapine) and papaverine are non-phenolic and stay in the organic layer.`,`Codeine is the 3-methyl ether of morphine and thebaine is the 3,6-dimethyl ether; most commercial codeine is made by methylating morphine.`,`Caffeine is purified by sublimation, which is possible because it is a non-basic alkaloid that sublimes at about 178 degrees C.`,`Hyoscine (scopolamine) differs from hyoscyamine by an epoxide bridge between C-6 and C-7; hyoscine is a CNS depressant while atropine is a CNS stimulant at high dose.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Quantitative and pharmacopoeial facts`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Typical official alkaloid content`],rows:[[`Opium`,`Not less than 9.5 pc anhydrous morphine`],[`Belladonna leaf`,`Not less than 0.3 pc total alkaloids`],[`Belladonna root`,`Not less than 0.4 pc total alkaloids`],[`Cinchona bark`,`Not less than 6.5 pc total alkaloids`],[`Ipecacuanha`,`Not less than 2.0 pc total alkaloids`],[`Nux vomica`,`Not less than 1.2 pc strychnine`],[`Ephedra`,`About 0.5 to 2.0 pc total alkaloids`]],caption:`Values follow the standard pharmacopoeial monographs commonly quoted in Indian teaching; individual editions may revise the limits.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Vinca alkaloids`,text:`Vincristine and vinblastine are dimeric indole (bisindole) alkaloids of Catharanthus roseus that bind tubulin and arrest mitosis in metaphase. Vincristine is more neurotoxic; vinblastine is more myelosuppressive. The yield is famously low, around a gram of vincristine from half a tonne of leaf.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Ergot alkaloids fall into three groups: water-soluble amine alkaloids (ergometrine), ergotamine group, and ergotoxine group. All share the lysergic acid nucleus.`,`Physostigmine (eserine) from Physostigma venenosum is a reversible carbamate cholinesterase inhibitor; pilocarpine from Pilocarpus is a direct muscarinic agonist. Both are miotics but by different mechanisms.`,`Colchicine from Colchicum autumnale is neutral, not basic, so it is not precipitated by most alkaloidal reagents; it binds tubulin at a site different from the vinca site.`,`Piperine of black pepper is a weakly basic acid amide and acts as a bioavailability enhancer by inhibiting glucuronidation and CYP enzymes.`,`Reserpine content of Rauwolfia serpentina root is about 0.05 to 0.2 percent; the root also contains ajmaline and ajmalicine used in arrhythmia and cerebral circulation.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Solanaceae tropane trio`,text:`Belladonna, Datura, Hyoscyamus — all Solanaceae, all tropane, all anisocytic (cruciferous) stomata. Belladonna is mostly hyoscyamine, Hyoscyamus and Datura metel are richer in hyoscine.`}]},{topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Volatile oils, also called essential oils or ethereal oils, are the odorous principles of plants. They evaporate completely at room temperature, they are steam distillable, and chemically they are mixtures of terpenoids together with some aromatic and aliphatic compounds.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Property`,`Volatile oil`,`Fixed oil`],rows:[[`Chemistry`,`Terpenoids, phenols, aldehydes`,`Glycerides of fatty acids`],[`Steam distillation`,`Distils over`,`Does not distil`],[`Stain on paper`,`Evaporates, no permanent stain`,`Permanent translucent stain`],[`Rancidity`,`Does not become rancid; resinifies`,`Becomes rancid`],[`Saponification`,`Not saponified by alkali`,`Saponified to soap and glycerol`],[`Odour`,`Strong, characteristic`,`Bland or faint`]]},{kind:`para`,text:`The building block of a terpenoid is isoprene, a five-carbon unit. The isoprene rule states that terpenoids are built from isoprene units joined head to tail, so their carbon count comes in multiples of five.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Isoprene units`,`Carbon atoms`,`Example`],rows:[[`Monoterpene`,`2`,`C10`,`Menthol, camphor, limonene, geraniol`],[`Sesquiterpene`,`3`,`C15`,`Zingiberene, artemisinin, farnesol`],[`Diterpene`,`4`,`C20`,`Taxol, phytol, vitamin A skeleton, forskolin`],[`Triterpene`,`6`,`C30`,`Squalene, glycyrrhetinic acid`],[`Tetraterpene`,`8`,`C40`,`Carotenoids`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Counting terpenes`,text:`Mono 10, Sesqui 15, Di 20, Tri 30, Tetra 40. Sesqui means "one and a half" — one and a half monoterpenes, hence C15.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Volatile oils are stored in oil glands, oil ducts (vittae in umbelliferous fruits), glandular trichomes or lysigenous cavities.`,`They are used as flavours, perfumes, carminatives, counter-irritants, antiseptics and as starting materials for semisynthesis.`,`They are optically active, have a high refractive index, and are soluble in alcohol and organic solvents but only sparingly in water.`,`They are stored in well-filled, tightly closed, light-resistant containers in a cool place because oxygen, light and heat cause resinification.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Volatile oil content in a crude drug`,text:`Volatile oil content of a crude drug is determined by hydrodistillation in a Clevenger apparatus and expressed as percent v/w. The graduated receiver lets you read the oil volume directly.`}]},{topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The reliable marks come from one big table: drug, source, family, part, chief constituent and its approximate percentage. After that come extraction methods and the evaluation constants.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Biological source`,`Family`,`Part used`,`Chief constituent (approx.)`],rows:[[`Clove`,`Syzygium aromaticum (Eugenia caryophyllus)`,`Myrtaceae`,`Dried flower bud`,`Eugenol 80-90 pc of the oil; oil 15-20 pc`],[`Cinnamon`,`Cinnamomum zeylanicum`,`Lauraceae`,`Dried inner bark`,`Cinnamaldehyde 60-75 pc; eugenol 5-10 pc`],[`Cassia`,`Cinnamomum cassia`,`Lauraceae`,`Bark`,`Cinnamaldehyde about 80 pc, no eugenol`],[`Peppermint`,`Mentha piperita`,`Labiatae`,`Dried leaf and top`,`Menthol 50-60 pc, menthone`],[`Eucalyptus`,`Eucalyptus globulus`,`Myrtaceae`,`Fresh leaf`,`Cineole (eucalyptol) not less than 70 pc`],[`Lemon`,`Citrus limon`,`Rutaceae`,`Fresh peel`,`Limonene about 90 pc, citral 3-5 pc`],[`Coriander`,`Coriandrum sativum`,`Umbelliferae`,`Dried ripe fruit`,`Coriandrol (d-linalool) 60-70 pc`],[`Fennel`,`Foeniculum vulgare`,`Umbelliferae`,`Dried ripe fruit`,`Anethole 50-60 pc, fenchone`],[`Caraway`,`Carum carvi`,`Umbelliferae`,`Dried ripe fruit`,`Carvone 50-60 pc, limonene`],[`Dill`,`Anethum graveolens (A. sowa)`,`Umbelliferae`,`Dried ripe fruit`,`Carvone; Indian dill also has dillapiole`],[`Cardamom`,`Elettaria cardamomum`,`Zingiberaceae`,`Dried ripe fruit / seed`,`Cineole and alpha-terpinyl acetate`],[`Ginger`,`Zingiber officinale`,`Zingiberaceae`,`Dried rhizome`,`Oil: zingiberene; pungency: gingerol, shogaol`],[`Turmeric`,`Curcuma longa`,`Zingiberaceae`,`Dried rhizome`,`Curcumin (colour); oil: turmerone`],[`Chenopodium`,`Chenopodium ambrosioides`,`Chenopodiaceae`,`Fresh flowering top`,`Ascaridole 60-80 pc`],[`Sandalwood`,`Santalum album`,`Santalaceae`,`Heartwood`,`Santalol not less than 90 pc`],[`Valerian`,`Valeriana wallichii, V. officinalis`,`Valerianaceae`,`Rhizome and root`,`Valepotriates, valerenic acid, bornyl acetate`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Chemical types of volatile oil`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Chemical group`,`Marker compound`,`Oil`],rows:[[`Hydrocarbon`,`Limonene, pinene`,`Lemon, turpentine`],[`Alcohol`,`Menthol, geraniol, santalol`,`Peppermint, rose, sandalwood`],[`Aldehyde`,`Cinnamaldehyde, citral`,`Cinnamon, lemongrass`],[`Ketone`,`Carvone, camphor, menthone`,`Caraway, camphor, peppermint`],[`Phenol`,`Eugenol, thymol`,`Clove, thyme, ajowan`],[`Phenolic ether`,`Anethole, safrole, myristicin`,`Fennel, anise, sassafras, nutmeg`],[`Oxide`,`Cineole, ascaridole`,`Eucalyptus, chenopodium`],[`Ester`,`Methyl salicylate, bornyl acetate`,`Wintergreen, valerian`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Extraction methods`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Method`,`How it works`,`Best suited to`],rows:[[`Hydro-distillation`,`Drug boiled in water, vapour condensed`,`Powdered, dense material like cinnamon bark`],[`Steam distillation`,`Live steam passed through the charge`,`Thermostable leafy drugs: peppermint, eucalyptus`],[`Water and steam distillation`,`Charge on a grid above boiling water`,`Material that cakes or scorches; ajowan, clove`],[`Expression (ecuelle, sponge, machine)`,`Cold mechanical pressing of peel`,`Citrus peel oils — heat-sensitive lemon, orange`],[`Enfleurage`,`Cold fat absorbs oil from fresh petals`,`Delicate flowers that keep producing oil: jasmine, tuberose`],[`Maceration in hot fat (digestion)`,`Warm fat absorbs the oil`,`Flowers whose oil is released on warming: rose`],[`Solvent extraction`,`Petroleum ether gives concrete, then absolute`,`Oleoresins and delicate florals`],[`Supercritical fluid (CO2)`,`CO2 above 31 deg C and 73 bar acts as solvent`,`Thermolabile, high-value oils; no solvent residue`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Why citrus oils are expressed`,text:`Citrus peel oils contain heat-sensitive aldehydes such as citral and non-volatile coumarins. Distillation would destroy the aroma, so lemon and orange oils are obtained by cold expression. Enfleurage is reserved for flowers such as jasmine and tuberose whose petals go on producing oil after picking.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Evaluation of volatile oils uses specific gravity, optical rotation, refractive index, solubility in a stated strength of alcohol, ester value, and assay of the marker constituent.`,`Phenol content (eugenol in clove oil) is measured by shaking with potassium hydroxide solution in a Cassia flask and reading the volume of unabsorbed oil.`,`Aldehyde and ketone content is measured by the bisulphite or hydroxylamine hydrochloride (oximation) method.`,`A congealing point test identifies oils rich in a crystallisable constituent, for example anethole in anise oil.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Cinnamon versus cassia`,text:`Both are rich in cinnamaldehyde, but only true cinnamon (Cinnamomum zeylanicum) contains appreciable eugenol. Cassia oil gives no eugenol reaction and its bark is thicker and coarser. Cinnamon bark is also the only official bark taken from coppiced shoots.`}]},{topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced material covers the biosynthetic routes, the quantitative constants, and the small structural facts that generate exam distractors.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Terpenoids arise from isopentenyl pyrophosphate. In the cytosol IPP comes from the mevalonic acid pathway (sesquiterpenes, triterpenes, sterols); in the plastid it comes from the deoxyxylulose phosphate (MEP) pathway (monoterpenes, diterpenes, carotenoids).`,`Phenylpropanoids such as eugenol, anethole, cinnamaldehyde and safrole are NOT terpenoids — they come from the shikimic acid pathway through phenylalanine, and have a C6-C3 skeleton.`,`Menthol is a monoterpene alcohol with three chiral centres, so eight stereoisomers exist; only l-menthol has the cooling activity, because it selectively activates the TRPM8 receptor.`,`Camphor is a bicyclic monoterpene ketone; borneol is the corresponding alcohol and is oxidised to camphor.`,`Ascaridole of chenopodium oil is a peroxide monoterpene and is explosive on heating with organic acids — it must never be distilled to dryness.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Volatile oil content in the drug`,`Marker assay limit`],rows:[[`Clove`,`About 15 to 20 pc v/w`,`Eugenol not less than 80 pc of the oil`],[`Cinnamon bark`,`About 0.5 to 1.0 pc`,`Cinnamaldehyde 60 to 75 pc of the oil`],[`Peppermint`,`About 1.0 to 2.5 pc`,`Total menthol not less than 50 pc`],[`Fennel`,`About 3 to 7 pc`,`Anethole about 50 to 60 pc`],[`Caraway`,`About 3 to 7 pc`,`Carvone not less than 50 pc`],[`Cardamom`,`About 4 to 8 pc`,`Cineole plus terpinyl acetate`],[`Eucalyptus oil`,`Oil is the drug`,`Cineole not less than 70 pc`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The two carvone drugs`,text:`Caraway contains d-carvone (dextrorotatory) and spearmint contains l-carvone. The two enantiomers smell completely different — caraway versus spearmint — which is the classic teaching example of chirality and odour.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Adulteration and detection in volatile oils`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Adulterant`,`Detection`],rows:[[`Alcohol`,`Water test — oil becomes turbid; or the fuchsin-sulphurous acid test`],[`Fixed oil`,`Permanent grease stain on paper; not soluble in 70 pc alcohol`],[`Kerosene / petroleum`,`Insoluble in 90 pc alcohol; low refractive index`],[`Turpentine oil`,`Raises optical rotation, distinct pinene odour on TLC`],[`Water`,`Turbidity, oil fails the clarity and solubility test`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Clove oil is heavier than water (specific gravity about 1.04 to 1.06) because of its high eugenol content — most volatile oils are lighter than water.`,`Cinnamon and cassia oils are also heavier than water; sandalwood oil is near unity.`,`Peppermint oil is a Labiatae drug with diacytic stomata; the leaf shows characteristic short one to eight celled glandular trichomes with an eight-celled head.`,`Valepotriates of valerian are unstable iridoid esters that decompose on storage into baldrinals, so aqueous extracts of fresh root differ from stored powder.`,`Ginger is inodorous when the oil is removed; its pungency is due to non-volatile gingerol, which converts to the more pungent shogaol on drying and to zingerone on further heating.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Oil of a drug versus the drug`,text:`Clove yields 15 to 20 percent of volatile oil and eugenol is 80 to 90 percent OF THAT OIL, not of the clove. Students who quote eugenol as 85 percent of clove lose the mark. Always state whether a percentage refers to the crude drug or to the oil.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Umbelliferous fruits`,text:`Coriander, fennel, caraway, dill, anise, ajowan, cumin — all Umbelliferae fruits with vittae (oil ducts). Linalool, anethole, carvone, carvone, anethole, thymol, cuminaldehyde in the same order.`}]},{topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Tannins are non-nitrogenous polyphenols that bind and precipitate proteins — that is why they tan hide into leather, taste astringent, and are used on burns and in diarrhoea. Resins are amorphous, brittle, non-nitrogenous solids formed by oxidation of terpenes; they are insoluble in water but dissolve in alcohol.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Astringent: contracts tissue and reduces secretion. Tannins do this by precipitating surface proteins.`,`Tannins are soluble in water and alcohol, give a blue-black or green-black colour with ferric chloride, and precipitate gelatin and alkaloids.`,`Resin: soluble in alcohol, ether and chloroform; insoluble in water; softens on heating and burns with a smoky flame.`,`Oleoresin: resin plus volatile oil (ginger, capsicum, turpentine).`,`Oleo-gum-resin: resin plus volatile oil plus gum (asafoetida, myrrh, gamboge).`,`Balsam: resin containing benzoic acid, cinnamic acid or their esters (benzoin, Peru balsam, tolu balsam, storax).`,`Glycoresin: resin combined with sugar (jalap, ipomoea, podophyllum contains a resin with lignan glycosides).`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Hydrolysable tannin`,`Condensed tannin`],rows:[[`Core`,`Sugar (usually glucose) esterified with phenolic acid`,`No sugar; flavan-3-ol units`],[`Hydrolysis`,`Split by acid, alkali or tannase`,`Not hydrolysed; gives red phlobaphenes`],[`Ferric chloride`,`Blue or blue-black`,`Green or green-black`],[`Bromine water`,`No precipitate`,`Precipitate formed`],[`Examples`,`Gallic acid and ellagic acid tannins`,`Catechins, proanthocyanidins`],[`Drugs`,`Myrobalan, gall, chebulic myrobalan, clove`,`Cinchona bark, catechu, tea, cinnamon`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Ferric chloride colour`,text:`Hydrolysable = Blue-Black (both start with B, and hydrolysable tannins hydrolyse to gallic acid). Condensed = Green (Condensed catechins, C for the green Catechu).`},{kind:`para`,text:`Two classic confirmatory tests for tannins are the goldbeater skin test and the gelatin test. Goldbeater skin is untreated ox caecum membrane; soaked in dilute hydrochloric acid, washed, then dipped in the tannin solution and washed again, it turns brown or black because tannin has bound to its protein. In the gelatin test, a 1 percent gelatin solution containing 10 percent sodium chloride gives a white precipitate with tannins.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Goldbeater skin`,text:`The goldbeater skin test distinguishes true tannins from pseudotannins. True tannins have a molecular weight roughly 500 to 3000 and can cross-link protein; pseudotannins such as gallic acid, catechin and chlorogenic acid are smaller, give a positive ferric chloride reaction but a NEGATIVE goldbeater skin test.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Tannins are used as astringents, in diarrhoea, as antidotes in alkaloid poisoning (they precipitate alkaloids), and on burns as tannic acid jelly.`,`They interfere with iron absorption and with protein digestion, which is why strong tea with meals is discouraged in anaemia.`,`Resins are used as purgatives (jalap, podophyllum), protectives (benzoin), and in varnishes and plasters (colophony).`]}]},{topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At exam level you need the sub-classes of hydrolysable tannin, a source table for the tannin drugs, and a full source-and-use table for the resins and resin combinations.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Sub-classes of tannin`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Sub-class`,`On hydrolysis gives`,`Typical drug`],rows:[[`Gallitannin (gallotannin)`,`Gallic acid + glucose`,`Nutgall (Quercus infectoria), rhubarb`],[`Ellagitannin`,`Ellagic acid + glucose`,`Myrobalan, pomegranate rind, eucalyptus`],[`Condensed (proanthocyanidin)`,`Phlobaphenes (red insoluble)`,`Catechu, cinchona, tea, wild cherry`],[`Pseudotannin`,`Not a true tannin, low MW`,`Gallic acid, catechin, chlorogenic acid, ipecacuanhic acid`]]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Biological source`,`Family`,`Part used`,`Chief constituent`,`Use`],rows:[[`Nutgall`,`Quercus infectoria (insect gall)`,`Fagaceae`,`Excrescence on twig`,`Gallotannic acid 50-70 pc`,`Astringent, source of tannic acid`],[`Myrobalan (harad)`,`Terminalia chebula`,`Combretaceae`,`Dried ripe fruit`,`Chebulinic acid, chebulagic acid 30-40 pc`,`Astringent, laxative, Triphala`],[`Black catechu`,`Acacia catechu`,`Leguminosae`,`Dried aqueous extract of heartwood`,`Catechin, catechutannic acid`,`Astringent, mouth wash`],[`Pale catechu (gambir)`,`Uncaria gambier`,`Rubiaceae`,`Dried extract of leaf and twig`,`Catechin, gambir-fluorescein`,`Astringent`],[`Ashoka`,`Saraca asoca`,`Leguminosae`,`Dried bark`,`Tannins, catechol`,`Uterine tonic`],[`Amla`,`Emblica officinalis`,`Euphorbiaceae`,`Fruit`,`Emblicanin, tannins, vitamin C`,`Antioxidant, astringent`],[`Bahera`,`Terminalia bellirica`,`Combretaceae`,`Fruit`,`Tannins, bellericanin`,`Astringent, Triphala`],[`Arjuna`,`Terminalia arjuna`,`Combretaceae`,`Stem bark`,`Arjunolic acid, tannins`,`Cardiotonic`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Resins and resin combinations`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Biological source`,`Family`,`Nature`,`Chief constituent`,`Use`],rows:[[`Podophyllum`,`Podophyllum hexandrum, P. peltatum`,`Berberidaceae`,`Dried rhizome/root, resin`,`Podophyllotoxin, peltatins`,`Antimitotic, genital warts, precursor of etoposide`],[`Ginger`,`Zingiber officinale`,`Zingiberaceae`,`Oleoresin of rhizome`,`Gingerol, shogaol, zingiberene`,`Carminative, antiemetic`],[`Asafoetida (hing)`,`Ferula asafoetida, F. foetida`,`Umbelliferae`,`Oleo-gum-resin from rhizome`,`Ferulic acid esters, asaresinotannol, sulphur compounds`,`Carminative, antispasmodic`],[`Benzoin`,`Styrax benzoin (Sumatra), S. tonkinensis (Siam)`,`Styracaceae`,`Balsamic resin`,`Coniferyl benzoate, cinnamic and benzoic acid`,`Expectorant inhalation, antiseptic, protective`],[`Colophony (rosin)`,`Pinus palustris and other Pinus species`,`Pinaceae`,`Residue after distilling turpentine`,`Abietic acid`,`Plasters, ointments, adhesive`],[`Cannabis`,`Cannabis sativa`,`Cannabinaceae`,`Dried flowering top, resin`,`Tetrahydrocannabinol, cannabidiol`,`Narcotic; antiemetic, analgesic (controlled)`],[`Jalap`,`Ipomoea purga`,`Convolvulaceae`,`Dried tuber, glycoresin`,`Convolvulin, jalapin`,`Drastic purgative`],[`Balsam of Peru`,`Myroxylon pereirae`,`Leguminosae`,`Balsam`,`Cinnamein (benzyl benzoate + benzyl cinnamate)`,`Antiseptic, scabies, parasiticide`],[`Balsam of Tolu`,`Myroxylon balsamum`,`Leguminosae`,`Balsam`,`Cinnamic and benzoic acid esters`,`Expectorant, flavour`],[`Storax`,`Liquidambar orientalis`,`Hamamelidaceae`,`Balsam`,`Storesin, cinnamic acid esters`,`Expectorant, in compound benzoin tincture`],[`Myrrh`,`Commiphora molmol`,`Burseraceae`,`Oleo-gum-resin`,`Commiphoric acids, volatile oil`,`Astringent mouth wash`],[`Guggul`,`Commiphora mukul`,`Burseraceae`,`Oleo-gum-resin`,`Guggulsterones E and Z`,`Hypolipidaemic`],[`Capsicum`,`Capsicum annuum, C. frutescens`,`Solanaceae`,`Oleoresin of fruit`,`Capsaicin`,`Counter-irritant, rubefacient`],[`Colocynth`,`Citrullus colocynthis`,`Cucurbitaceae`,`Dried pulp of fruit`,`Colocynthin, cucurbitacins`,`Drastic purgative`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Sumatra versus Siam benzoin`,text:`Siam benzoin (Styrax tonkinensis) contains benzoic acid and its esters but essentially NO cinnamic acid. Sumatra benzoin (Styrax benzoin) contains both cinnamic and benzoic acids. The classic distinguishing test is heating with potassium permanganate: Sumatra gives the odour of benzaldehyde from cinnamic acid, Siam does not.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Resin, oleoresin, gum-resin`,text:`Ginger and capsicum are OLEORESINS (resin + volatile oil, no gum). Asafoetida, myrrh and gamboge are OLEO-GUM-RESINS (they also contain gum, so they form emulsions with water). Colophony is a pure resin, the non-volatile residue left after turpentine oil is distilled off.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Asafoetida gives the Rathke test — a scraping treated with sulphuric acid then alcohol gives a red-violet colour with ferulic acid.`,`Podophyllotoxin is a lignan, not a terpene; it is the parent of the semisynthetic anticancer drugs etoposide and teniposide, which act on topoisomerase II.`,`Cannabis resin gives the Beam test — a violet colour with alcoholic potassium hydroxide.`,`Colophony gives the Halphen-Hicks test and the Liebermann-Storch reaction (violet with acetic anhydride and sulphuric acid).`,`Turpentine oil is the volatile part distilled from crude turpentine oleoresin; colophony is what remains.`]}]},{topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced tier is the chemistry that generates the colour tests, the quantitative estimation of tannins, and the toxicology and stereochemistry of the resin drugs.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Gallotannin of nutgall is penta-m-digalloyl glucose; hydrolysis by tannase or dilute acid liberates gallic acid and glucose.`,`Ellagitannins yield hexahydroxydiphenic acid, which spontaneously lactonises to ellagic acid — the acid you actually see in the assay.`,`Condensed tannins are polymers of flavan-3-ols (catechin, epicatechin). Boiling with acid gives red insoluble phlobaphenes and anthocyanidins, so they are also called proanthocyanidins.`,`The Vanillin-hydrochloric acid test gives a pink to red colour with condensed tannins and catechins, and is used to quantify them.`,`Tannin content is officially determined by the hide-powder method: total phenols minus phenols not adsorbed by hide powder gives the tannin fraction.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Test`,`Reagent`,`Result / meaning`],rows:[[`Ferric chloride`,`Dilute FeCl3`,`Blue-black = hydrolysable; green-black = condensed`],[`Goldbeater skin`,`Ox caecum membrane, HCl wash`,`Brown-black stain = true tannin only`],[`Gelatin`,`1 pc gelatin with 10 pc NaCl`,`White precipitate = tannin`],[`Bromine water`,`Saturated bromine water`,`Precipitate = condensed tannin`],[`Vanillin-HCl`,`Vanillin in conc. HCl`,`Pink-red = catechin / condensed tannin`],[`Match-stick (phloroglucinol)`,`Wood splinter dipped in extract + HCl`,`Pink = catechin`],[`Lead acetate`,`Basic lead acetate`,`Precipitate = tannins and phenolics`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Pseudotannins list`,text:`Gallic acid (nutgall), catechin (catechu, cinchona, acacia), chlorogenic acid (coffee, nux vomica), ipecacuanhic acid (ipecac). All give ferric chloride colours but fail the goldbeater skin test because they cannot cross-link protein.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Resin chemistry and safety`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Podophyllum resin (podophyllin) is applied topically only. Podophyllotoxin arrests cells in metaphase by binding tubulin at the colchicine site; etoposide, made from it, acts instead on topoisomerase II — the mechanism changes with the semisynthetic modification.`,`Indian podophyllum (Podophyllum hexandrum) yields a higher resin content, about 6 to 12 percent, than American podophyllum, about 3 to 6 percent, and is richer in podophyllotoxin.`,`Jalap resin is 90 percent soluble in alcohol; the convolvulin fraction is insoluble in ether while jalapin (scammonin) is ether soluble — the standard way to distinguish jalap from Indian jalap (Operculina turpethum).`,`Capsaicin is a vanillylamide, not a terpene; it depletes substance P from sensory C fibres, which is why repeated application causes desensitisation.`,`Cannabis potency depends on tetrahydrocannabinolic acid, which decarboxylates to active THC on smoking or heating. Charas is the resin, ganja the flowering top, bhang the dried leaf.`,`Colophony causes contact dermatitis and is a common allergen in adhesive plasters; abietic acid is the sensitiser.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Tannic acid is not one substance`,text:`Pharmacopoeial tannic acid is a mixture of galloyl esters of glucose obtained from nutgalls, not a single compound. Prolonged use on large burn surfaces was abandoned because absorbed tannic acid caused hepatic necrosis.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Balsam checklist`,text:`A true balsam must contain benzoic or cinnamic acid or their esters: Benzoin, Peru, Tolu, Storax. Copaiba and Canada balsam are called balsams by trade custom but are not true balsams — Canada balsam is an oleoresin used as a microscopy mountant.`}]},{topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Extraction is the process of pulling the wanted constituents out of a crude drug with a suitable solvent, leaving the inert plant tissue behind. Isolation is the next step: separating one pure compound from the mixture the extraction gave you.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Menstruum: the solvent used for extraction.`,`Marc: the exhausted plant residue left after extraction.`,`Miscella: the solvent carrying the dissolved constituents.`,`Menstruum choice follows "like dissolves like": water for gums, tannins and glycosides; alcohol for most constituents; ether or petroleum ether for fats, waxes and volatile oils.`,`Size reduction increases surface area and speeds extraction, but too fine a powder blocks the percolator bed.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Method`,`How it is done`,`Best for`],rows:[[`Maceration`,`Drug soaked in closed vessel 3 to 7 days with shaking`,`Soft, unorganised drugs; thermolabile constituents`],[`Percolation`,`Solvent flows slowly through a packed column of drug`,`Potent tinctures and extracts; near-complete extraction`],[`Digestion`,`Maceration with gentle heat, about 40 to 60 deg C`,`Hard, dense material where heat does no harm`],[`Infusion`,`Drug steeped in hot or cold water for a short time`,`Water-soluble, heat-labile principles`],[`Decoction`,`Drug boiled with water for 15 to 20 minutes`,`Hard woody drugs, roots, barks; water-soluble stable actives`],[`Soxhlet (continuous hot)`,`Solvent recycles by siphoning over the drug`,`Small samples; economical use of solvent`],[`Counter-current extraction`,`Drug and solvent move in opposite directions`,`Large scale, concentrated extract, less solvent`],[`Supercritical fluid (CO2)`,`CO2 above 31 deg C and 73.8 bar as solvent`,`Thermolabile, high value; leaves no solvent residue`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Maceration versus percolation`,text:`Maceration is a batch, equilibrium process, so some drug is always left unextracted; it is chosen when the drug is thermolabile or forms a slimy mass on percolation. Percolation is continuous and gives near-complete extraction, which is why potent tinctures such as belladonna tincture are made by percolation.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Water methods by heat`,text:`Infusion = steep briefly. Decoction = boil hard. Digestion = warm and soak. Maceration = cold and soak.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Once the extract is made, the constituents are separated by fractionation — shaking the extract with solvents of increasing polarity so each class of compound moves into the solvent that suits it — and then purified by chromatography and crystallisation.`}]},{topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Two things are examined here: the advantage each method offers over the others, and the phytochemical screening test for each class of constituent.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Method`,`Main advantage`,`Main limitation`],rows:[[`Maceration`,`Simple, no heat, cheap apparatus`,`Incomplete, slow, large solvent volume`],[`Percolation`,`More complete than maceration, continuous fresh solvent`,`Needs careful packing; channelling ruins it`],[`Soxhlet`,`Repeated fresh solvent contact with little solvent`,`Prolonged heat degrades thermolabile actives`],[`Decoction`,`Cheap, extracts water-soluble actives fully`,`Destroys volatile and heat-labile constituents`],[`Counter-current`,`High concentration extract, minimum solvent, no heat`,`Costly plant, needs uniform particle size`],[`Supercritical CO2`,`No solvent residue, low temperature, tunable selectivity`,`High capital cost, poor for polar constituents`],[`Ultrasound / microwave assisted`,`Rapid, small solvent volume`,`Local heating and possible degradation`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Soxhlet is not always superior`,text:`Soxhlet uses far less solvent than maceration and keeps presenting fresh solvent to the drug, but the extract sits in a boiling flask for hours. For thermolabile glycosides and volatile constituents it is the wrong choice, and cold maceration or supercritical CO2 is preferred.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Fractionation and isolation`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Prepare a total alcoholic extract and concentrate it.`,`Suspend in water and partition successively with petroleum ether (fats, waxes, sterols), chloroform or dichloromethane (aglycones, alkaloid bases, terpenoids), ethyl acetate (flavonoids, phenolics, some glycosides) and n-butanol (saponins, polar glycosides).`,`Screen each fraction by TLC and by the class tests to locate the activity.`,`Purify the active fraction by column chromatography — silica gel for normal phase, reversed phase C18 for polar compounds, Sephadex LH-20 for size and for tannin removal.`,`Crystallise, then confirm identity by melting point, UV, IR, mass spectrometry and NMR.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Constituent class`,`Screening test`,`Positive result`],rows:[[`Alkaloids`,`Mayer, Dragendorff, Wagner, Hager`,`Cream, orange, brown, yellow precipitate`],[`Cardiac glycosides`,`Keller-Kiliani; Legal; Kedde; Baljet`,`Reddish-brown ring; pink; purple; orange`],[`Anthraquinones`,`Borntrager / modified Borntrager`,`Pink to red ammoniacal layer`],[`Saponins`,`Foam test; haemolysis test`,`Persistent honeycomb froth; haemolysis`],[`Flavonoids`,`Shinoda (Mg + conc. HCl); alkali test`,`Pink-magenta; yellow deepening with NaOH`],[`Tannins`,`Ferric chloride; gelatin; goldbeater skin`,`Blue/green-black; white precipitate; brown stain`],[`Steroids and triterpenes`,`Liebermann-Burchard; Salkowski`,`Blue-green; red lower chloroform layer`],[`Carbohydrates`,`Molisch; Fehling; Benedict`,`Violet ring; brick-red precipitate`],[`Proteins and amino acids`,`Biuret; Ninhydrin; Millon`,`Violet; purple; brick red`],[`Fixed oils and fats`,`Filter paper stain; saponification`,`Permanent grease spot; soap formed`],[`Coumarins`,`Alkali + UV light`,`Blue or green fluorescence`],[`Volatile oils`,`Steam distillation; paper spot evaporates`,`Oil layer in receiver; no permanent stain`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Salkowski versus Liebermann-Burchard`,text:`Both detect the steroid nucleus. Salkowski uses chloroform plus concentrated sulphuric acid and the red colour appears in the LOWER acid layer; Liebermann-Burchard uses acetic anhydride plus sulphuric acid and gives a blue-green colour that develops through pink and violet.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Molisch reagent is alpha-naphthol in alcohol; concentrated sulphuric acid is layered underneath to give the violet ring — it is a general test for all carbohydrates.`,`Fehling and Benedict detect reducing sugars only; sucrose is negative until it is hydrolysed.`,`Ninhydrin gives a purple colour with alpha-amino acids and a yellow colour with proline and hydroxyproline.`,`Selective adsorbents matter: polyamide and Sephadex LH-20 remove tannins, which otherwise mask activity in bioassays.`]}]},{topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced tier is the physical chemistry behind supercritical extraction, the specialised chromatographic techniques, and the standardisation of the extract that results.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Carbon dioxide has a critical temperature of about 31.1 degrees C and a critical pressure of about 73.8 bar. Above both it is a supercritical fluid with gas-like diffusivity and liquid-like density.`,`Selectivity is tuned by changing density: raising pressure raises the solvating power, so a pressure programme fractionates the extract in one run.`,`Supercritical CO2 is non-polar, so polar constituents need a modifier (entrainer) such as 1 to 10 percent ethanol or methanol.`,`CO2 is non-flammable, non-toxic, cheap and leaves no residue on decompression, which makes the process ideal for food and pharmaceutical grade extracts such as decaffeinated coffee and hop extract.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Concentration in extract at equilibrium: C_extract = C_drug x K / (1 + K)`,caption:`K is the partition coefficient of the constituent between menstruum and marc. Because a single maceration never drives K to infinity, repeated small volumes of solvent extract more than one large volume — the basis of multiple extraction and of percolation.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Technique`,`Principle`,`Typical phytochemical use`],rows:[[`Column chromatography (silica)`,`Adsorption, normal phase`,`Bulk separation of alkaloids, terpenoids`],[`Flash chromatography`,`Silica under positive pressure`,`Rapid preparative separation`],[`Reversed phase HPLC (C18)`,`Partition, polar mobile phase`,`Assay and isolation of glycosides, flavonoids`],[`Gel permeation (Sephadex LH-20)`,`Size exclusion`,`Tannin removal, separation of oligomers`],[`Ion exchange`,`Charge`,`Separation of alkaloid salts, amino acids`],[`Countercurrent chromatography (CCC)`,`Liquid-liquid partition, no solid support`,`Isolation without irreversible adsorption loss`],[`Preparative TLC`,`Adsorption on a thick layer`,`Small-scale isolation, purity check`],[`GC and GC-MS`,`Volatility and partition`,`Volatile oil profiling`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Why countercurrent chromatography for natural products`,text:`Countercurrent chromatography has no solid stationary phase, so there is no irreversible adsorption and sample recovery approaches 100 percent. That matters when only milligrams of a valuable alkaloid or a labile glycoside are available.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Extracts are classified as infusion or decoction (aqueous, freshly prepared), tincture (alcoholic, dilute, usually 1 in 5 or 1 in 10), liquid extract (1 part drug in 1 part liquid), soft extract, and dry extract.`,`Standardisation of an extract uses a marker: an active marker when the therapeutic principle is known (sennosides in senna), or an analytical marker when it is not (a characteristic but inactive compound used only for identity).`,`Native (native drug extract ratio, DER) expresses parts of crude drug used per part of extract, for example DER 4:1.`,`Chromatographic fingerprinting by HPTLC or HPLC is the accepted way to prove batch-to-batch consistency of a multi-constituent extract.`,`Enfleurage, expression and distillation are extraction methods reserved for volatile oils; they are not used for glycosides or alkaloids.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Percolation faults`,text:`Channelling, caking and air locks are the three percolation faults. They arise from uneven packing, too fine a powder, or wetting the powder inadequately before packing. All of them let solvent bypass the drug bed, so the percolate is weak even though the marc is not exhausted.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Polarity ladder for fractionation`,text:`Pet ether, chloroform, ethyl acetate, n-butanol, water — Pretty Cool Extraction By Water. Fats first, sugars and saponins last.`}]},{topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This topic pulls together three small but reliably examined areas: pharmaceutical aids of fibre origin (cotton, jute, silk, wool, nylon) with their surgical uses, the types of adulteration of crude drugs and how each is detected, and the newer sources of drugs — marine organisms and plant tissue culture.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Fibre`,`Biological source`,`Family`,`Chemical nature`,`Use`],rows:[[`Cotton`,`Gossypium herbaceum and other species`,`Malvaceae`,`Cellulose, epidermal hair of seed`,`Absorbent cotton, swabs, dressings`],[`Jute`,`Corchorus capsularis, C. olitorius`,`Tiliaceae`,`Cellulose with lignin (bast fibre)`,`Sacking; treated jute in dressings`],[`Silk`,`Bombyx mori (silkworm cocoon)`,`Bombycidae`,`Protein — fibroin and sericin`,`Non-absorbable suture, sieve cloth`],[`Wool`,`Ovis aries fleece`,`Bovidae`,`Protein — keratin`,`Surgical wool, source of wool fat`],[`Nylon`,`Synthetic polyamide`,`Not applicable`,`Polyamide polymer`,`Non-absorbable suture, filter fabric`],[`Regenerated cellulose (rayon)`,`Wood pulp, chemically regenerated`,`Not applicable`,`Cellulose`,`Oxidised cellulose haemostat, dressings`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Identification of fibres`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Burning test: cellulose fibres (cotton, jute) burn quickly with the smell of burning paper; protein fibres (silk, wool) smell of burning hair and leave a crushable black bead; nylon melts and forms a hard bead.`,`Cotton under the microscope is a flattened, twisted, ribbon-like unicellular hair with thick walls and a lumen. It is negative to lignin tests.`,`Jute gives a yellow colour with aniline sulphate and a pink to red colour with phloroglucinol and hydrochloric acid, because it is lignified. Cotton gives neither.`,`Silk dissolves in a cold ammoniacal copper oxide solution and gives xanthoproteic and Millon reactions; it dissolves in concentrated hydrochloric acid.`,`Wool has an overlapping scaly cuticle under the microscope, contains sulphur, and gives a black lead sulphide with lead acetate and alkali.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Cotton versus jute in one test`,text:`Phloroglucinol with concentrated hydrochloric acid gives a pink-red colour only with lignified fibres. Jute is lignified and answers positively; absorbent cotton is pure cellulose and does not. That single test is the standard way to detect jute in a cotton dressing.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Absorbent cotton is prepared by removing the fat, wax and pigment from raw cotton with alkali under pressure, followed by bleaching and washing. It must sink in water within 10 seconds and leave very little residue on ignition. Ordinary raw cotton floats, because its natural wax repels water.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Suture`,`Type`,`Made from`],rows:[[`Catgut`,`Absorbable`,`Submucosa of sheep intestine (collagen)`],[`Polyglycolic acid / polyglactin`,`Absorbable synthetic`,`Glycolide and lactide polymers`],[`Silk`,`Non-absorbable`,`Bombyx mori fibroin`],[`Nylon`,`Non-absorbable`,`Synthetic polyamide`],[`Linen and cotton thread`,`Non-absorbable`,`Cellulose fibre`],[`Stainless steel wire`,`Non-absorbable`,`Metal`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Absorbable list is short`,text:`Absorbable sutures: catgut, collagen, polyglycolic acid, polyglactin, polydioxanone. Everything else in the usual list — silk, nylon, linen, polyester, steel — is non-absorbable.`}]},{topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Adulteration means supplying a drug that does not conform to its official standard. It may be deliberate (sophistication) or accidental, and the pharmacopoeial evaluation tests are designed so that each type is caught by a specific test.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type of adulteration`,`What is done`,`How it is detected`],rows:[[`Inferior commercial variety`,`A related but weaker species substituted`,`Morphology, leaf constants, marker assay`],[`Superficially similar substance`,`A cheap look-alike with no activity`,`Microscopy and chemical tests`],[`Exhausted drug`,`Drug already extracted, then dried and sold`,`Low extractive values, low water-soluble ash, weak odour and taste`],[`Artificially manufactured substitute`,`A fabricated imitation of the drug`,`Microscopy, melting point, solubility, chemical identity`],[`Addition of worthless heavy material`,`Sand, stones, lead shot, iron filings, water`,`Total ash and acid-insoluble ash, loss on drying`],[`Adulteration of powders`,`Powdered exhausted or foreign material mixed in`,`Powder microscopy, lycopodium spore count`],[`Substitution with synthetic principle`,`Synthetic compound added to boost assay`,`Optical rotation, chromatographic fingerprint`]]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Genuine drug`,`Common adulterant / substitute`],rows:[[`Saffron (Crocus sativus)`,`Dried florets of Carthamus tinctorius; exhausted saffron`],[`Belladonna leaf`,`Ailanthus, Phytolacca, Solanum leaves`],[`Digitalis purpurea leaf`,`Digitalis thapsi, Verbascum thapsus, Inula conyza`],[`Clove`,`Exhausted (deoiled) clove, mother cloves, clove stalks`],[`Nutmeg`,`Artificially carved wooden nutmegs`],[`Cinnamon`,`Cassia bark`],[`Senna leaf`,`Argel leaf (Solenostemma argel), Dodonaea, Cassia obovata`],[`Honey`,`Commercial invert sugar, jaggery syrup`],[`Wax and beeswax`,`Paraffin wax, stearic acid, Japan wax`],[`Asafoetida`,`Colophony, gypsum, red clay, wheat flour`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The exhausted drug signature`,text:`An exhausted drug looks right and passes microscopy, because the tissue is untouched. It is caught by the numbers: alcohol- and water-soluble extractive values fall, water-soluble ash falls, and the assay of the marker constituent fails. Sometimes the drug is recoloured, so an added-dye test may also be positive.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Substitution is not always fraud`,text:`Accidental substitution happens through wrong collection, careless drying, imperfect preparation and storage failures. Deliberate substitution for profit is called sophistication. Examiners ask which type an example belongs to, so read the intent in the stem.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Marine drugs`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Marine product`,`Source organism`,`Class / use`],rows:[[`Cytarabine (Ara-C)`,`Cryptotethya crypta sponge (arabinosyl nucleosides)`,`Antileukaemic nucleoside analogue`],[`Vidarabine (Ara-A)`,`Sponge nucleosides`,`Antiviral`],[`Trabectedin (ecteinascidin)`,`Ecteinascidia turbinata (tunicate)`,`Anticancer alkaloid`],[`Ziconotide`,`Conus magus cone snail`,`N-type calcium channel blocker, analgesic`],[`Eribulin (halichondrin analogue)`,`Halichondria okadai sponge`,`Anticancer, tubulin inhibitor`],[`Agar, alginate, carrageenan`,`Red and brown algae`,`Pharmaceutical aids`],[`Cod and shark liver oil`,`Gadus and shark species`,`Vitamins A and D; squalene`],[`Spirulina`,`Arthrospira platensis`,`Protein and beta-carotene nutraceutical`],[`Chitin and chitosan`,`Crustacean shells`,`Excipient, wound dressing, hypolipidaemic`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Plant tissue culture grows cells, tissues or organs aseptically on a defined nutrient medium; Murashige and Skoog medium is the standard.`,`Callus is an unorganised mass of dividing cells; suspension culture is callus dispersed in liquid medium; a hairy root culture is induced by Agrobacterium rhizogenes.`,`Auxin favours root formation and callus; cytokinin favours shoot formation. A high auxin to cytokinin ratio gives roots, a low ratio gives shoots.`,`Micropropagation gives disease-free, genetically uniform clones and is independent of season and geography.`,`Elicitation, precursor feeding and immobilisation are used to raise the yield of secondary metabolites in culture.`]}]},{topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced tier is the specific tests that identify each surgical aid, the pharmacopoeial limits, and the commercially successful examples of biotechnological drug production.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Fibre`,`Specific chemical test`,`Result`],rows:[[`Cotton`,`Iodine + 80 pc sulphuric acid (cuprammonium)`,`Blue colour, swells and dissolves`],[`Jute`,`Aniline sulphate; phloroglucinol + HCl`,`Yellow; pink-red (lignin present)`],[`Silk`,`Millon reagent; conc. HCl`,`Brick red; dissolves (no sulphur, no scales)`],[`Wool`,`Lead acetate + NaOH; Millon`,`Black lead sulphide (sulphur); brick red`],[`Nylon`,`Formic acid; heat`,`Dissolves in formic acid; melts to a hard bead`],[`Regenerated cellulose`,`Iodine + sulphuric acid`,`Blue, but structureless under microscope`]],caption:`The sulphur test is the decisive one between silk and wool — only wool (keratin) gives lead sulphide.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Absorbent cotton wool must sink within 10 seconds when a small piece is dropped on water, must yield not more than about 0.5 percent sulphated ash, and must show no fluorescence other than that of the trace impurity limit.`,`Absorbent gauze, ribbon gauze, lint, tow and bandage cloth are the standard surgical dressings; a medicated dressing carries a drug such as iodine or a fibrinolysin.`,`Oxidised cellulose and calcium alginate are absorbable haemostatic dressings; they are left in the wound and are absorbed.`,`Catgut is sterilised in a tubing fluid and packed in alcohol; plain catgut is absorbed in about 7 to 10 days and chromicised catgut, tanned with chromic salts, lasts about 20 to 40 days.`,`Suture size is expressed by the USP numbering: the more zeros the finer the thread, so 5-0 is much finer than 2-0.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Sinking time of absorbent cotton`,text:`The pharmacopoeial sinking test — absorbent cotton must sink within 10 seconds — is a direct measure of how completely the natural cotton wax was removed during purification. Raw cotton floats indefinitely.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Quantifying adulteration`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Foreign organic matter in most official crude drugs is limited to not more than 2 percent w/w.`,`A rise in acid-insoluble ash beyond the monograph limit points specifically to sand and earth, because silica does not dissolve in dilute hydrochloric acid.`,`The lycopodium spore method quantifies how much of a powdered drug is genuine when the adulterant cannot be separated physically.`,`DNA barcoding using rbcL, matK, ITS2 and psbA-trnH identifies the species in a powdered or processed sample where morphology is useless.`,`HPTLC fingerprinting detects both substitution and the addition of a synthetic marker, because the whole profile, not one peak, must match.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`High total ash does not prove adulteration`,text:`Total ash includes physiological ash from the plant itself, which varies with soil and season. Only acid-insoluble ash isolates the added earthy matter. Reporting adulteration on total ash alone is the standard wrong answer.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Tissue culture success`,`Product`,`Note`],rows:[[`Lithospermum erythrorhizon`,`Shikonin`,`First commercial plant cell culture product`],[`Coptis japonica`,`Berberine`,`High-yielding suspension culture`],[`Panax ginseng`,`Ginsenosides`,`Commercial root culture in Japan and Korea`],[`Taxus species`,`Paclitaxel`,`Plant cell fermentation now a commercial route`],[`Digitalis lanata`,`Digoxin from beta-methyldigitoxin`,`Biotransformation by cultured cells`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Five types of adulteration`,text:`I-S-E-A-H: Inferior variety, Superficially similar, Exhausted drug, Artificial substitute, Heavy worthless material.`}]}],t=[{id:`q-pg-classification-evaluation-1`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which system of classification groups botanically unrelated crude drugs together on the basis of a shared constituent or a shared therapeutic action?`,options:[`Taxonomic`,`Chemical only`,`Alphabetical`,`Both chemical and pharmacological`],answer:3,explanation:`Chemical classification groups drugs by chief constituent and pharmacological classification groups them by therapeutic action, so unrelated families sit together (senna, aloe and rhubarb are all anthraquinone purgatives from three different families). Taxonomic classification does the opposite — it keeps related plants together — and alphabetical order is arbitrary rather than a grouping of unrelated plants by a shared property.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pg-classification-evaluation-2`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which ash value is the most reliable indicator of adulteration of a crude drug with sand and earthy matter?`,options:[`Acid-insoluble ash`,`Total ash`,`Water-soluble ash`,`Sulphated ash`],answer:0,explanation:`Sand and soil are largely silica, which does not dissolve in dilute hydrochloric acid, so it remains in the acid-insoluble ash. Total ash is a tempting answer but it lumps physiological ash from the plant with added dirt and therefore cannot distinguish the two.`},{id:`q-pg-classification-evaluation-3`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Stomatal index is preferred over stomatal number for the evaluation of a leaf drug because`,options:[`It is easier to count under low magnification`,`It is a ratio and therefore constant for a species irrespective of leaf age and growing conditions`,`It is unaffected by the method of clearing the leaf`,`It can be determined on powdered drug alone`],answer:1,explanation:`Stomatal index expresses stomata as a percentage of total epidermal cells, so any factor that changes both counts cancels out, leaving a species constant. Stomatal number is stomata per square millimetre and rises or falls with humidity, light and leaf maturity, which makes it unreliable for identity.`},{id:`q-pg-classification-evaluation-4`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Anisocytic (cruciferous) stomata are characteristic of`,options:[`Senna`,`Datura and belladonna`,`Digitalis`,`Peppermint`],answer:1,explanation:`Solanaceous leaf drugs — belladonna, datura and hyoscyamus — show anisocytic stomata, in which three subsidiary cells of unequal size surround the pore. Senna is paracytic (rubiaceous), digitalis is anomocytic and peppermint is diacytic, so all three distractors belong to different stomatal types.`,tag:`Structure ID`},{id:`q-pg-classification-evaluation-5`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In a lycopodium spore determination, 25 mg of powdered drug was mixed with 5 mg of lycopodium. Counting gave 440 drug particles against 44 lycopodium spores. The number of drug particles per mg is`,options:[`47000`,`188000`,`94000`,`376000`],answer:1,explanation:`Particles per mg = (N x 94000 x W) / (S x M) = (440 x 94000 x 5) / (44 x 25) = 206800000 / 1100 = 188000 particles per mg. The constant 94000 is the number of spores in 1 mg of standard lycopodium; forgetting to multiply by the 5 mg of lycopodium taken gives the common wrong answer of 37600.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pg-classification-evaluation-6`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Alexandrian senna is distinguished from Tinnevelly senna most reliably by`,options:[`Sennoside content`,`Stomatal type, which differs between the two`,`Vein-islet number, which is higher in Alexandrian senna`,`Acid-insoluble ash`],answer:2,explanation:`Vein-islet number is about 25.0 to 29.5 for Cassia acutifolia (Alexandrian) and about 19.5 to 22.5 for Cassia angustifolia (Tinnevelly), and it is independent of leaf age. Sennoside content overlaps between the two and varies with cultivation, and both species share paracytic stomata, so neither of those separates them.`},{id:`q-pg-classification-evaluation-7`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A crude drug rich in volatile oil needs its water content determined. The most appropriate method is`,options:[`Loss on drying at 105 degrees C`,`Incineration at 450 degrees C`,`Determination of water-soluble extractive`,`Azeotropic distillation with toluene`],answer:3,explanation:`Azeotropic distillation with toluene collects water in a graduated receiver while the volatile oil stays with the toluene, giving a water-specific figure. Loss on drying is the tempting choice but it would also drive off the volatile oil and grossly over-read the moisture content.`},{id:`q-pg-classification-evaluation-8`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The bitterness value of a drug is expressed relative to a solution of`,options:[`Gentiopicrin, 1 g in 1000 mL`,`Standard saponin, 1 g in 500 mL`,`Quinine hydrochloride, 1 g in 2000 mL`,`Sucrose, 1 g in 100 mL`],answer:2,explanation:`Bitterness value is defined in units equivalent to the bitterness of a solution containing 1 g of quinine hydrochloride in 2000 mL of water. Gentiopicrin is a bitter principle rather than the reference standard, and standard saponin (Saponinum album) is the reference for the haemolytic index, not for bitterness.`},{id:`q-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-1`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The biological source of tragacanth is`,options:[`Astragalus gummifer`,`Acacia senegal`,`Sterculia urens`,`Cyamopsis tetragonoloba`],answer:0,explanation:`Tragacanth is the dried gummy exudate of Astragalus gummifer and related species of Leguminosae. Acacia senegal gives acacia (gum arabic), Sterculia urens gives karaya or Indian tragacanth, and Cyamopsis tetragonoloba gives guar gum — all commonly confused with tragacanth in MCQs.`},{id:`q-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-2`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Acacia is preferred as an emulsifying agent while tragacanth is used as a suspending agent because`,options:[`Acacia has a higher viscosity than tragacanth`,`Tragacanth contains an oxidase enzyme`,`Acacia is insoluble in alcohol`,`Acacia dissolves completely in water whereas tragacanth only swells into a stiff gel`],answer:3,explanation:`Acacia forms a true, completely soluble mucilage of low viscosity that adsorbs at the oil-water interface, which is exactly what an emulsifier needs. Tragacanth swells rather than dissolves and produces a very high viscosity gel, so it thickens and suspends but is a poor emulsifier; note also that the oxidase enzyme is present in acacia, not tragacanth.`},{id:`q-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-3`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which starch shows the largest granules with a markedly eccentric hilum and concentric striations?`,options:[`Maize starch`,`Potato starch`,`Rice starch`,`Wheat starch`],answer:1,explanation:`Potato starch granules are the largest of the official starches, up to about 100 micrometres, ovoid, with an eccentric hilum and clear concentric striations. Rice starch has the smallest granules in compound masses, maize is polygonal with a central cleft hilum, and wheat shows a bimodal mixture of large lenticular and small spherical granules.`,tag:`Structure ID`},{id:`q-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-4`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The Fiehe test on honey gives a cherry-red colour. This indicates`,options:[`Genuine unheated honey`,`Contamination with pollen from a toxic species`,`The presence of hydroxymethylfurfural from added invert sugar or overheating`,`A high fructose to glucose ratio`],answer:2,explanation:`Fiehe test uses resorcinol in hydrochloric acid and detects hydroxymethylfurfural, which is generated when sucrose is acid-inverted commercially or when honey is overheated. It is therefore an adulteration test; a negative result, not a red colour, is what genuine unheated honey gives.`},{id:`q-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-5`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A fixed oil has a saponification value of 195.4 mg KOH per g and an acid value of 1.4 mg KOH per g. Its ester value is`,options:[`196.8 mg KOH per g`,`194.0 mg KOH per g`,`139.6 mg KOH per g`,`1.4 mg KOH per g`],answer:1,explanation:`Ester value = saponification value minus acid value = 195.4 - 1.4 = 194.0 mg KOH per g. Adding the two values to get 196.8 is the standard error, and it has no analytical meaning because the saponification value already includes the free fatty acid contribution.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-6`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The Halphen test is used to detect`,options:[`Sesame oil`,`Cottonseed oil`,`Arachis oil`,`Linseed oil`],answer:1,explanation:`Halphen test uses carbon disulphide and sulphur and gives a red colour with cottonseed oil, because of its cyclopropenoid fatty acids. Sesame oil is detected by the Baudouin test (crimson with furfural and HCl, due to sesamolin) and arachis oil by the Bellier turbidity test, which are the two most common distractors.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-7`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Castor oil is unique among common fixed oils in being soluble in an equal volume of alcohol and in having an acetyl value of about 144 to 150. Both facts are explained by`,options:[`The hydroxyl group of ricinoleic acid`,`A very high degree of unsaturation`,`Its very short chain fatty acids`,`The presence of ricin in the oil`],answer:0,explanation:`Ricinoleic acid carries a free hydroxyl group at C-12, which raises polarity (hence alcohol solubility) and provides the site that is acetylated in the acetyl value determination. Unsaturation would show up in the iodine value rather than the acetyl value, and ricin is a toxalbumin that stays in the seed cake and never enters the oil.`},{id:`q-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-8`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Coconut oil has a saponification value of about 250 to 264, much higher than that of castor or linseed oil. This is because coconut oil contains`,options:[`More unsaturated fatty acids`,`Shorter chain fatty acids such as lauric acid, giving more ester groups per gram`,`Free hydroxyl groups on its fatty acid chains`,`A higher proportion of unsaponifiable matter`],answer:1,explanation:`Saponification value is the milligrams of KOH needed per gram of oil, so it is inversely related to mean fatty acid chain length: shorter chains mean more triglyceride molecules and more ester bonds per gram. Unsaturation is measured by iodine value and free hydroxyls by acetyl value, so neither explains a high saponification value.`},{id:`q-pg-glycosides-1`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Keller-Kiliani test is used to identify`,options:[`The unsaturated lactone ring of cardenolides`,`Free anthraquinones`,`The steroidal nucleus of saponins`,`The 2-deoxy sugar of cardiac glycosides`],answer:3,explanation:`Keller-Kiliani uses glacial acetic acid with a trace of ferric chloride layered over concentrated sulphuric acid and gives a reddish-brown ring because of the 2-deoxy sugars digitoxose and cymarose. The lactone ring is what Baljet, Legal and Kedde detect, which is the commonest confusion in this set.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pg-glycosides-2`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The biological source of squill is`,options:[`Digitalis lanata`,`Strophanthus kombe`,`Urginea indica`,`Thevetia neriifolia`],answer:2,explanation:`Squill is the dried slices of the bulb of Urginea indica (Indian squill) or Drimia maritima, and its glycosides scillaren A and B are bufadienolides. Digitalis lanata gives lanatosides, Strophanthus gives K-strophanthoside and ouabain, and Thevetia gives thevetin — all cardiac drugs but none of them squill.`},{id:`q-pg-glycosides-3`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Aloe requires the modified Borntrager test rather than the ordinary Borntrager test because barbaloin is`,options:[`An O-glycoside that hydrolyses too fast`,`A C-glycoside present in the reduced anthrone form`,`A dianthrone that is insoluble in ammonia`,`A saponin that interferes with the colour`],answer:1,explanation:`Barbaloin is a C-glycoside, so ordinary acid hydrolysis cannot cleave the carbon-carbon sugar bond, and it exists in the reduced anthrone state which gives no pink colour with ammonia. Ferric chloride in the modified test supplies the oxidation to anthraquinone and assists cleavage, so the ammoniacal layer then turns pink.`},{id:`q-pg-glycosides-4`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which glycoside gives a poor or negative Keller-Kiliani response despite being a cardiac glycoside?`,options:[`Digitoxin`,`Ouabain`,`Digoxin`,`K-strophanthoside`],answer:1,explanation:`Ouabain (G-strophanthin) carries rhamnose, which is not a 2-deoxy sugar, so the reddish-brown ring fails to develop. Digitoxin and digoxin each carry three digitoxose units and K-strophanthoside carries cymarose, and all three of those are 2-deoxy sugars that answer the test strongly.`,tag:`Exception`},{id:`q-pg-glycosides-5`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A persistent honeycomb froth on shaking an aqueous plant extract, together with haemolysis of a red cell suspension, indicates`,options:[`Tannins`,`Anthraquinone glycosides`,`Saponins`,`Cyanogenetic glycosides`],answer:2,explanation:`Saponins lower surface tension, giving stable froth, and complex with membrane cholesterol, lysing erythrocytes — hence the foam and haemolysis tests together. Tannins precipitate proteins rather than foam, and anthraquinones are identified by the pink ammoniacal Borntrager layer.`},{id:`q-pg-glycosides-6`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Digitoxin is best described as`,options:[`A secondary glycoside formed by enzymatic loss of glucose from purpurea glycoside A`,`A primary glycoside of Digitalis purpurea`,`A bufadienolide with a six-membered lactone`,`An aglycone with no sugar residues`],answer:0,explanation:`The primary glycoside in the living leaf is purpurea glycoside A; the plant enzyme digipurpidase removes the terminal glucose during drying to give digitoxin, making it a secondary glycoside. Digitoxin is a cardenolide with a five-membered lactone and still carries three digitoxose units, so the other options are wrong on both counts.`},{id:`q-pg-glycosides-7`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Black mustard is a rubefacient while white mustard is not, because sinigrin on hydrolysis by myrosin yields`,options:[`Allyl isothiocyanate, which is volatile and vesicant`,`p-hydroxybenzyl isothiocyanate, which is non-volatile`,`Hydrocyanic acid and benzaldehyde`,`Sinalbin and glucose`],answer:0,explanation:`Sinigrin of Brassica nigra is hydrolysed by myrosin to volatile allyl isothiocyanate, the vesicant responsible for the counter-irritant action. White mustard contains sinalbin, which yields the non-volatile p-hydroxybenzyl isothiocyanate, so it is pungent to taste but does not blister the skin; hydrocyanic acid and benzaldehyde come from amygdalin, a cyanogenetic glycoside.`},{id:`q-pg-glycosides-8`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Excessive intake of liquorice produces oedema, sodium retention and hypokalaemia because glycyrrhetinic acid`,options:[`Directly stimulates the mineralocorticoid receptor with high affinity`,`Inhibits 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, allowing cortisol to act on mineralocorticoid receptors`,`Blocks renal potassium reabsorption`,`Inhibits carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule`],answer:1,explanation:`Glycyrrhetinic acid, the aglycone of glycyrrhizin, inhibits 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2, which normally converts cortisol to inactive cortisone in the kidney; the accumulated cortisol then activates mineralocorticoid receptors and produces pseudo-aldosteronism. It is not itself a receptor agonist, which is the usual wrong choice.`},{id:`q-pg-alkaloids-1`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Dragendorff reagent, used as a general test for alkaloids, is`,options:[`Potassium mercuric iodide`,`Iodine in potassium iodide`,`Potassium bismuth iodide`,`Saturated picric acid`],answer:2,explanation:`Dragendorff reagent is potassium bismuth iodide and gives an orange to reddish-brown precipitate. Potassium mercuric iodide is Mayer reagent (cream precipitate), iodine in potassium iodide is Wagner reagent, and saturated picric acid is Hager reagent (yellow precipitate).`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pg-alkaloids-2`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following pairs of nucleus and drug is correct?`,options:[`Imidazole — Rauwolfia`,`Purine — Ipecacuanha`,`Quinoline — Ephedra`,`Tropane — Belladonna`],answer:3,explanation:`Belladonna contains hyoscyamine and atropine, which are tropane esters. Rauwolfia is indole, ipecac is isoquinoline (emetine and cephaeline), and ephedra contains ephedrine, a protoalkaloid amine with nitrogen outside any ring — pilocarpine is the standard imidazole example and quinine the quinoline one.`},{id:`q-pg-alkaloids-3`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The thalleioquin test, in which bromine water followed by ammonia gives an emerald-green colour, is specific for`,options:[`Cinchonine`,`Strychnine`,`Quinine`,`Emetine`],answer:2,explanation:`Thalleioquin formation requires the 6-methoxy group on the quinoline ring, which quinine and quinidine possess. Cinchonine and cinchonidine lack the methoxy group and give no green colour, which is exactly how the two pairs of cinchona alkaloids are told apart.`},{id:`q-pg-alkaloids-4`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A deep red colour with ferric chloride that is not discharged by hydrochloric acid is the classic identification test for`,options:[`Ergot`,`Opium, due to meconic acid`,`Nux vomica, due to brucine`,`Cinchona, due to quinic acid`],answer:1,explanation:`Opium alkaloids exist as salts of meconic acid, and meconic acid gives a red ferric complex that persists in the presence of hydrochloric acid, unlike the red colours of acetates and thiocyanates. Ergot is identified by the blue Van Urk colour, and brucine in nux vomica by the orange-yellow it gives with concentrated nitric acid.`},{id:`q-pg-alkaloids-5`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The alkaloid actually present in the living belladonna plant is`,options:[`l-Hyoscyamine, which racemises to atropine during extraction`,`Atropine, the racemic form`,`Hyoscine only`,`Apoatropine`],answer:0,explanation:`The plant synthesises l-hyoscyamine; the racemic dl form, called atropine, is produced by racemisation during drying and extraction, and it is roughly half as active as the natural levo isomer. Hyoscine is present in belladonna only in small amounts and predominates instead in Datura metel and Hyoscyamus.`},{id:`q-pg-alkaloids-6`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Although quinine is structurally a quinoline alkaloid, its biosynthetic precursor amino acid is`,options:[`Tryptophan`,`Ornithine`,`Anthranilic acid`,`Tyrosine`],answer:0,explanation:`Quinine arises from tryptophan through strictosidine, the common intermediate of terpenoid indole alkaloids, and the indole nucleus is later rearranged into the quinoline system. Anthranilic acid is the precursor of the quinazoline and acridine alkaloids such as vasicine, and tyrosine gives the isoquinoline group.`,tag:`Exception`},{id:`q-pg-alkaloids-7`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In the extraction of opium, morphine is separated from papaverine and narcotine by taking advantage of the fact that morphine is`,options:[`Non-basic and therefore not extracted by acid`,`Phenolic and therefore soluble in aqueous alkali`,`Volatile and can be sublimed`,`The only alkaloid soluble in chloroform`],answer:1,explanation:`Morphine has a free phenolic hydroxyl, so in alkali it forms a water-soluble phenolate and stays in the aqueous layer while the non-phenolic bases papaverine and narcotine pass into the organic solvent. Sublimation is the purification used for caffeine, not for morphine.`},{id:`q-pg-alkaloids-8`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Colchicine is not precipitated by most alkaloidal reagents because it`,options:[`Is a quaternary ammonium compound`,`Has too high a molecular weight`,`Is present only as a glycoside`,`Is neutral, its nitrogen being part of an amide rather than a basic amine`],answer:3,explanation:`The nitrogen of colchicine is an acetamido nitrogen, so the molecule is effectively neutral and does not form the insoluble salts that Mayer, Dragendorff and Hager reagents rely on. Quaternary alkaloids such as berberine and tubocurarine are permanently charged and are readily precipitated, which is why that distractor is wrong.`},{id:`q-pg-volatile-oils-1`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A sesquiterpene contains how many carbon atoms?`,options:[`C10`,`C20`,`C15`,`C30`],answer:2,explanation:`A sesquiterpene is built from three isoprene units, giving C15 — zingiberene and farnesol are examples. C10 is a monoterpene, C20 a diterpene and C30 a triterpene, and the prefix sesqui, meaning one and a half, refers to one and a half monoterpenes.`},{id:`q-pg-volatile-oils-2`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The chief constituent of eucalyptus oil is`,options:[`Eugenol`,`Anethole`,`Carvone`,`Cineole`],answer:3,explanation:`Eucalyptus oil from Eucalyptus globulus contains not less than about 70 percent cineole (eucalyptol), an oxide monoterpene. Eugenol belongs to clove, anethole to fennel and anise, and carvone to caraway and dill.`},{id:`q-pg-volatile-oils-3`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Lemon and orange peel oils are obtained by cold expression rather than distillation because`,options:[`The oil is heavier than water and would not separate`,`The aldehydes such as citral are heat sensitive and the aroma would be spoiled`,`The peel contains too much water for distillation`,`The oil is a fixed oil and cannot be distilled`],answer:1,explanation:`Citrus peel oils are dominated by limonene together with heat-labile aldehydes such as citral, and distillation degrades them and destroys the characteristic aroma, so ecuelle, sponge or machine expression is used. Citrus oils are volatile oils lighter than water, so the other options are factually wrong.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pg-volatile-oils-4`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Enfleurage is the method of choice for`,options:[`Jasmine and tuberose flowers`,`Cinnamon bark`,`Clove buds`,`Chenopodium tops`],answer:0,explanation:`Enfleurage uses cold fat to absorb oil from delicate flowers such as jasmine and tuberose, which continue to produce oil for hours after picking and whose oil is destroyed by heat. Cinnamon, clove and chenopodium are all robust enough for distillation, and chenopodium in particular is hydrodistilled with care because of its peroxide ascaridole.`},{id:`q-pg-volatile-oils-5`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`True cinnamon is distinguished from cassia bark chiefly by the presence in cinnamon of appreciable`,options:[`Cinnamaldehyde`,`Coumarin`,`Eugenol`,`Anethole`],answer:2,explanation:`Both barks are rich in cinnamaldehyde, so that constituent cannot separate them; only Cinnamomum zeylanicum yields appreciable eugenol, roughly 5 to 10 percent of the oil. Anethole is a fennel and anise constituent and has nothing to do with either bark.`},{id:`q-pg-volatile-oils-6`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Eugenol, anethole and cinnamaldehyde are grouped together as`,options:[`Monoterpenes from the mevalonate pathway`,`Sesquiterpene lactones`,`Oxide terpenoids`,`Phenylpropanoids from the shikimic acid pathway`],answer:3,explanation:`These compounds have a C6-C3 skeleton and are derived from phenylalanine through the shikimic acid pathway, so despite occurring in volatile oils they are not terpenoids at all. Monoterpenes such as menthol and limonene come instead from isopentenyl pyrophosphate, which in plastids is supplied by the deoxyxylulose phosphate pathway.`},{id:`q-pg-volatile-oils-7`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Caraway smells of caraway and spearmint smells of mint although both oils are rich in carvone. The explanation is that`,options:[`The two oils contain different amounts of carvone`,`Spearmint carvone is present as a glycoside`,`Caraway contains d-carvone and spearmint l-carvone, and the enantiomers smell different`,`Caraway carvone is oxidised to carvacrol during drying`],answer:2,explanation:`Carvone exists as two enantiomers, and olfactory receptors are chiral, so d-carvone is perceived as caraway and l-carvone as spearmint. Concentration differences change intensity, not the quality of the odour, so differing carvone content cannot account for two entirely different smells.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pg-volatile-oils-8`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Clove oil is one of the few volatile oils that is heavier than water, with a specific gravity of about 1.04 to 1.06. This is attributed to its high content of`,options:[`Eugenol`,`Limonene`,`Cineole`,`Bornyl acetate`],answer:0,explanation:`Eugenol, a phenolic phenylpropanoid, makes up 80 to 90 percent of clove oil and is denser than water, so the oil sinks. Hydrocarbon-rich oils such as those containing limonene are markedly lighter than water, which is why most volatile oils float.`},{id:`q-pg-tannins-resins-1`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Condensed tannins characteristically give which colour with ferric chloride solution?`,options:[`Blue or blue-black`,`Violet`,`Green or green-black`,`Yellow`],answer:2,explanation:`Condensed tannins, being catechol derivatives built from flavan-3-ol units, give a green to green-black colour with ferric chloride. Hydrolysable tannins, which yield gallic or ellagic acid, give the blue to blue-black colour, and that pair is the most frequently examined contrast in this topic.`},{id:`q-pg-tannins-resins-2`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Asafoetida is classified as`,options:[`A pure resin`,`An oleoresin`,`A balsam`,`An oleo-gum-resin`],answer:3,explanation:`Asafoetida from Ferula species contains resin, volatile oil and gum, so it is an oleo-gum-resin and forms an emulsion with water. Ginger and capsicum are oleoresins with no gum, colophony is a pure resin, and a balsam must contain benzoic or cinnamic acid or their esters.`},{id:`q-pg-tannins-resins-3`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Gallic acid, catechin and chlorogenic acid give a positive ferric chloride reaction but a negative goldbeater skin test. They are therefore classified as`,options:[`Hydrolysable tannins`,`Condensed tannins`,`Pseudotannins`,`Phlobaphenes`],answer:2,explanation:`Pseudotannins are low molecular weight phenolics that colour with ferric chloride but are too small to cross-link protein, so they do not stain goldbeater skin or tan hide. Phlobaphenes are the opposite — the red insoluble polymers formed when condensed tannins are boiled with acid.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pg-tannins-resins-4`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The biological source of podophyllum, the source of podophyllotoxin, is`,options:[`Podophyllum hexandrum, Berberidaceae`,`Berberis aristata, Berberidaceae`,`Ipomoea purga, Convolvulaceae`,`Commiphora mukul, Burseraceae`],answer:0,explanation:`Podophyllum is the dried rhizome and root of Podophyllum hexandrum (Indian) or P. peltatum (American), family Berberidaceae, and its resin contains podophyllotoxin. Ipomoea purga is jalap, a glycoresin purgative, and Commiphora mukul is guggul, a hypolipidaemic oleo-gum-resin.`},{id:`q-pg-tannins-resins-5`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Sumatra benzoin can be distinguished from Siam benzoin because only Sumatra benzoin contains`,options:[`Benzoic acid`,`Cinnamic acid and its esters`,`Coniferyl benzoate`,`Vanillin`],answer:1,explanation:`Siam benzoin from Styrax tonkinensis contains benzoic acid and its esters but essentially no cinnamic acid, whereas Sumatra benzoin from Styrax benzoin contains both; heating Sumatra benzoin with potassium permanganate liberates the almond odour of benzaldehyde from the cinnamic acid. Benzoic acid, coniferyl benzoate and traces of vanillin occur in both, so none of them discriminates.`},{id:`q-pg-tannins-resins-6`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Podophyllotoxin arrests cells in metaphase by binding tubulin, yet its semisynthetic derivative etoposide acts by`,options:[`Binding the vinca site on tubulin more strongly`,`Inhibiting topoisomerase II`,`Intercalating into DNA`,`Inhibiting dihydrofolate reductase`],answer:1,explanation:`Chemical modification of podophyllotoxin to etoposide abolishes tubulin binding and creates a topoisomerase II inhibitor that stabilises the cleavable complex and causes DNA strand breaks. Assuming that a semisynthetic derivative keeps the parent mechanism is the trap here.`},{id:`q-pg-tannins-resins-7`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`On hydrolysis, ellagitannins yield hexahydroxydiphenic acid, which is not isolated as such because it`,options:[`Is oxidised immediately to gallic acid`,`Polymerises to phlobaphenes`,`Is reduced to catechin`,`Spontaneously lactonises to ellagic acid`],answer:3,explanation:`Hexahydroxydiphenic acid closes to a double lactone spontaneously on release, and the compound recovered from the hydrolysate is ellagic acid. Phlobaphene formation is a property of condensed, not hydrolysable, tannins, so that distractor belongs to the other class entirely.`},{id:`q-pg-tannins-resins-8`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which statement about colophony is correct?`,options:[`It is the volatile fraction distilled from crude turpentine`,`It is an oleo-gum-resin of Pinus species`,`It is a balsam containing cinnamic acid esters`,`It is the non-volatile residue left after turpentine oil is distilled off, and abietic acid is its chief constituent`],answer:3,explanation:`Colophony, or rosin, is the brittle residue left in the still after the volatile turpentine oil has been distilled from the crude oleoresin of Pinus species, and abietic acid is its main acid and its chief contact allergen. It contains no gum and no cinnamic acid, so it is neither an oleo-gum-resin nor a balsam.`},{id:`q-pg-extraction-isolation-1`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The exhausted plant residue left after extraction is called the`,options:[`Marc`,`Menstruum`,`Miscella`,`Percolate`],answer:0,explanation:`The marc is the spent drug residue. The menstruum is the solvent used, the miscella is the solvent carrying the dissolved constituents, and the percolate is the liquid collected from a percolator.`},{id:`q-pg-extraction-isolation-2`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which extraction process involves boiling the drug with water for about 15 to 20 minutes?`,options:[`Infusion`,`Digestion`,`Maceration`,`Decoction`],answer:3,explanation:`Decoction boils the drug with water and suits hard, woody roots and barks whose actives are water soluble and heat stable. Infusion only steeps the drug briefly, digestion applies gentle warmth around 40 to 60 degrees C, and maceration is carried out cold.`},{id:`q-pg-extraction-isolation-3`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Percolation is preferred over maceration for preparing potent tinctures because`,options:[`Fresh solvent continuously displaces the saturated solvent, giving nearly complete extraction`,`It requires less skilled packing of the drug`,`It avoids the use of alcohol`,`It is carried out at a higher temperature`],answer:0,explanation:`In percolation, fresh menstruum keeps arriving at the drug bed so the concentration gradient is never allowed to fall to zero, and extraction approaches completion. Maceration is an equilibrium batch process that always leaves drug unextracted; percolation is not hotter and it demands more careful packing, not less.`},{id:`q-pg-extraction-isolation-4`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Salkowski test for steroids is performed with`,options:[`Acetic anhydride and concentrated sulphuric acid, giving a blue-green colour`,`Magnesium turnings and concentrated hydrochloric acid, giving a pink colour`,`Alpha-naphthol and concentrated sulphuric acid, giving a violet ring`,`Chloroform and concentrated sulphuric acid, giving a red colour in the lower layer`],answer:3,explanation:`Salkowski uses chloroform layered over concentrated sulphuric acid and the red colour appears in the lower acid layer. Acetic anhydride with sulphuric acid is Liebermann-Burchard, magnesium with HCl is the Shinoda flavonoid test, and alpha-naphthol with sulphuric acid is Molisch for carbohydrates.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pg-extraction-isolation-5`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`During successive solvent fractionation of an alcoholic extract, saponins and polar glycosides are most likely to concentrate in the`,options:[`n-Butanol fraction`,`Petroleum ether fraction`,`Chloroform fraction`,`Ethyl acetate fraction`],answer:0,explanation:`n-Butanol is the most polar of the water-immiscible solvents in the usual ladder, so it takes up saponins and highly glycosylated compounds. Petroleum ether removes fats, waxes and sterols first, chloroform takes aglycones and alkaloid bases, and ethyl acetate takes flavonoids and moderately polar phenolics.`},{id:`q-pg-extraction-isolation-6`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Supercritical carbon dioxide extraction operates above a critical temperature and pressure of approximately`,options:[`10 degrees C and 20 bar`,`100 degrees C and 220 bar`,`31 degrees C and 74 bar`,`55 degrees C and 5 bar`],answer:2,explanation:`Carbon dioxide has a critical temperature of about 31.1 degrees C and a critical pressure of about 73.8 bar, and above both it behaves as a supercritical fluid with liquid-like solvating power and gas-like diffusivity. The near-ambient critical temperature is precisely why the method suits thermolabile constituents.`},{id:`q-pg-extraction-isolation-7`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Countercurrent chromatography is particularly valued for natural product isolation because`,options:[`There is no solid stationary phase, so irreversible adsorption is avoided and recovery approaches 100 percent`,`It gives the highest theoretical plate count of any technique`,`It can be run without any solvent`,`It separates purely on molecular size`],answer:0,explanation:`Countercurrent chromatography partitions solutes between two immiscible liquid phases, so nothing sticks irreversibly to silica and the whole sample can be recovered — decisive when only milligrams of a labile glycoside exist. Size-based separation is gel permeation, and its efficiency is lower, not higher, than HPLC.`},{id:`q-pg-extraction-isolation-8`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Soxhlet extraction is inappropriate for a thermolabile glycoside chiefly because`,options:[`It uses too much solvent`,`It cannot be used with organic solvents`,`The extract remains in a boiling flask and is repeatedly heated for hours`,`The siphon dilutes the extract excessively`],answer:2,explanation:`The advantage of Soxhlet is that a small solvent volume is recycled, but the price is that everything already extracted stays in the boiling flask under continuous heat for the whole run, so labile compounds degrade. Solvent economy is a strength of the method, not a weakness, so excessive solvent use is the wrong criticism.`},{id:`q-pg-fibres-adulteration-1`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The biological source of silk used as a surgical suture is`,options:[`Gossypium herbaceum`,`Bombyx mori`,`Ovis aries`,`Corchorus capsularis`],answer:1,explanation:`Silk is the fibre from the cocoon of the silkworm Bombyx mori and is a protein made of fibroin with sericin. Gossypium gives cotton, Ovis aries gives wool, and Corchorus gives jute, all of which are also examined as pharmaceutical aids.`},{id:`q-pg-fibres-adulteration-2`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Absorbent cotton, when a small piece is dropped on the surface of water, must sink within`,options:[`10 seconds`,`2 seconds`,`60 seconds`,`5 minutes`],answer:0,explanation:`The pharmacopoeial sinking test requires absorbent cotton to sink within 10 seconds, which shows that the natural cotton wax has been removed during purification. Raw, unpurified cotton floats indefinitely because its wax repels water.`},{id:`q-pg-fibres-adulteration-3`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Jute present as an adulterant in a cotton dressing is detected because jute alone gives a pink-red colour with`,options:[`Iodine and sulphuric acid`,`Millon reagent`,`Phloroglucinol and hydrochloric acid`,`Lead acetate and sodium hydroxide`],answer:2,explanation:`Phloroglucinol with concentrated hydrochloric acid gives a pink-red colour with lignin, and jute is a lignified bast fibre while absorbent cotton is essentially pure cellulose. Iodine with sulphuric acid stains all cellulose blue, Millon detects protein fibres, and lead acetate with alkali detects the sulphur of wool.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pg-fibres-adulteration-4`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A consignment of a crude drug shows normal microscopy and normal total ash but markedly reduced alcohol-soluble and water-soluble extractive values. The most likely fault is`,options:[`Addition of sand and earth`,`Substitution with a superficially similar drug`,`Supply of an exhausted drug`,`An artificially manufactured substitute`],answer:2,explanation:`An exhausted drug has already been extracted, so its tissue and microscopy are intact but the soluble matter is gone and the extractive values fall. Sand would have raised the acid-insoluble ash, and a similar-looking substitute or a manufactured imitation would have been caught by microscopy.`},{id:`q-pg-fibres-adulteration-5`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which of the following is an absorbable suture?`,options:[`Catgut`,`Silk`,`Nylon`,`Linen thread`],answer:0,explanation:`Catgut, prepared from the collagen of the submucosa of sheep intestine, is digested in the tissues and is the classic absorbable suture; polyglycolic acid and polyglactin are its synthetic counterparts. Silk, nylon and linen thread all remain in the tissue and must be removed, so they are non-absorbable.`},{id:`q-pg-fibres-adulteration-6`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Wool can be distinguished from silk by a test based on the fact that only wool`,options:[`Gives a brick-red colour with Millon reagent`,`Dissolves in concentrated hydrochloric acid`,`Burns with the smell of burning hair`,`Contains sulphur and gives black lead sulphide with lead acetate and alkali`],answer:3,explanation:`Keratin of wool is rich in cystine, so boiling with alkali and lead acetate produces black lead sulphide, whereas the fibroin of silk has no appreciable sulphur. Both are proteins, so both give the Millon reaction and both smell of burnt hair on ignition, which makes those options useless for discrimination.`},{id:`q-pg-fibres-adulteration-7`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Cytarabine, an antileukaemic drug, was developed from arabinosyl nucleosides originally isolated from`,options:[`A cone snail, Conus magus`,`A marine sponge, Cryptotethya crypta`,`A tunicate, Ecteinascidia turbinata`,`A brown alga, Laminaria species`],answer:1,explanation:`The unusual arabinose-containing nucleosides of the Caribbean sponge Cryptotethya crypta led to cytarabine and vidarabine. Conus magus gave the analgesic peptide ziconotide, Ecteinascidia turbinata gave trabectedin, and Laminaria is the source of alginates, not nucleosides.`},{id:`q-pg-fibres-adulteration-8`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In plant tissue culture, a high auxin to cytokinin ratio in the medium promotes`,options:[`Shoot formation`,`Somatic embryogenesis only`,`Loss of totipotency`,`Root formation`],answer:3,explanation:`A high auxin to cytokinin ratio drives root initiation and callus proliferation, while a low ratio, that is relatively more cytokinin, drives shoot formation — the classic Skoog and Miller observation. Totipotency is a property of the cell and is not lost by adjusting hormone ratios.`},{id:`q-pg-classification-evaluation-9`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following is an unorganised drug?`,options:[`Ipecacuanha root`,`Senna leaflet`,`Cinchona bark`,`Acacia gum`],answer:3,explanation:`Unorganised drugs have no cellular structure because they are dried exudates, extracts, oils or latices; acacia is the dried gummy exudate of the stem and branches of Acacia senegal. Ipecac root, senna leaflet and cinchona bark are organised drugs whose tissues can be identified under the microscope.`},{id:`q-pg-classification-evaluation-10`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The foaming index of a crude drug measures its content of`,options:[`Saponins`,`Mucilage`,`Tannins`,`Volatile oil`],answer:0,explanation:`Foaming index records the ability of a decoction to produce a persistent 1 cm layer of froth, which is caused by surface-active saponins. Mucilage raises viscosity without giving a stable honeycomb froth, and tannins and volatile oils have no comparable foaming action.`},{id:`q-pg-classification-evaluation-11`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In a foaming index determination the tube containing 4 mL of the decoction was the first to show a persistent 1 cm layer of foam. The foaming index is`,options:[`250`,`4000`,`40`,`25`],answer:0,explanation:`Foaming index = 1000 / a, where a is the volume in mL of the decoction in the tube showing 1 cm of persistent foam, so 1000 / 4 = 250. The value 4000 comes from multiplying instead of dividing, and 40 would correspond to a 25 mL tube.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pg-classification-evaluation-12`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Palisade ratio is defined as`,options:[`The number of vein-islets per square millimetre of lamina`,`The percentage of stomata among the total epidermal cells`,`The average number of palisade cells lying beneath one epidermal cell`,`The number of veinlet terminations per square millimetre of lamina`],answer:2,explanation:`Palisade ratio counts how many palisade cells lie under a single upper epidermal cell, and it is determined by counting across four contiguous epidermal cells. Vein-islet number and veinlet termination number are counts per square millimetre of lamina, and the percentage of stomata among epidermal cells is the stomatal index.`},{id:`q-pg-classification-evaluation-13`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The pigeon emetic method is the biological assay used for`,options:[`Insulin`,`Vitamin D`,`Digitalis`,`Antibiotics`],answer:2,explanation:`Digitalis is bioassayed by the pigeon emetic method, and also by cat and guinea-pig methods, because its potency depends on a mixture of glycosides rather than one measurable marker. Insulin is assayed by the rabbit blood sugar or mouse convulsion method, vitamin D by the rat line test, and antibiotics by cup-plate diffusion.`},{id:`q-pg-classification-evaluation-14`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Incineration of 2.0 g of a powdered drug left 0.24 g of total ash. Boiling this ash with dilute hydrochloric acid and igniting the insoluble residue left 0.03 g. The total ash and the acid-insoluble ash respectively are`,options:[`12.0 pc and 12.5 pc`,`24.0 pc and 3.0 pc`,`1.2 pc and 0.15 pc`,`12.0 pc and 1.5 pc`],answer:3,explanation:`Both values are expressed on the weight of drug taken: total ash = (0.24 / 2.0) x 100 = 12.0 percent and acid-insoluble ash = (0.03 / 2.0) x 100 = 1.5 percent. Expressing the acid-insoluble residue as a percentage of the ash instead of the drug gives the wrong figure of 12.5 percent.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pg-classification-evaluation-15`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A cleared leaf preparation showed 30 stomata and 120 ordinary epidermal cells in the same field. The stomatal index is`,options:[`25`,`20`,`30`,`12`],answer:1,explanation:`Stomatal index = [S / (E + S)] x 100 = [30 / (120 + 30)] x 100 = 20. Dividing the stomata by the epidermal cells alone gives 25, the standard error, because the denominator must include the stomata themselves.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pg-classification-evaluation-16`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which locus is routinely used as a DNA barcode to identify the plant species present in a powdered herbal sample?`,options:[`Cytochrome c oxidase I (COI)`,`16S ribosomal RNA gene`,`matK`,`The CYP3A4 gene`],answer:2,explanation:`The plastid genes matK and rbcL, together with ITS2 and the psbA-trnH spacer, are the accepted plant barcoding loci and work on powdered material where morphology is useless. COI is the standard animal barcode and 16S ribosomal RNA is used for bacteria, so neither identifies a plant.`},{id:`q-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-9`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Isabgol, used as a bulk-forming laxative, is the`,options:[`Endosperm of the seed of Cyamopsis tetragonoloba`,`Dried exudate of Sterculia urens`,`Dried mucilage of red algae`,`Husk of the seed of Plantago ovata`],answer:3,explanation:`Isabgol is the epidermal husk stripped from the seeds of Plantago ovata, family Plantaginaceae, and its swelling index of not less than 40 gives it the bulk-forming action. The seed endosperm of Cyamopsis tetragonoloba is guar gum, the exudate of Sterculia urens is karaya, and red algal mucilage is agar.`},{id:`q-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-10`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The chief constituent of spermaceti is`,options:[`Cetyl palmitate`,`Myricyl palmitate`,`Myricyl cerotate`,`Cholesteryl esters`],answer:0,explanation:`Spermaceti, from Physeter macrocephalus, is largely cetyl palmitate. Myricyl palmitate is the main ester of beeswax and myricyl cerotate of carnauba wax, while cholesteryl esters make up wool fat, which is why lanolin behaves as a sterol-rich wax rather than a simple fatty ester.`},{id:`q-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-11`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which analytical constant is determined to assess the rancidity of a stored fixed oil?`,options:[`Iodine value`,`Acetyl value`,`Acid value`,`Saponification value`],answer:2,explanation:`Rancidity liberates free fatty acids, and the acid value measures exactly that as milligrams of potassium hydroxide needed to neutralise the free acid in 1 g of oil. Iodine value reports unsaturation, acetyl value reports free hydroxyl groups, and saponification value reflects the mean fatty acid chain length.`},{id:`q-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-12`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Karaya gum can be distinguished from tragacanth because karaya`,options:[`Is an acetylated polysaccharide and smells of acetic acid`,`Dissolves completely in cold water`,`Gives a blue colour with iodine solution`,`Contains an oxidase enzyme`],answer:0,explanation:`Karaya, the exudate of Sterculia urens, is a partly acetylated polysaccharide, so it slowly liberates acetic acid and carries a vinegary odour; it also stains red with ruthenium red. Neither karaya nor tragacanth dissolves completely — both swell into gels — and the oxidase enzyme is a property of acacia.`},{id:`q-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-13`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Adulteration of ghee with coconut oil is detected by determining the`,options:[`Reichert-Meissl value`,`Iodine value`,`Acetyl value`,`Polenske value`],answer:3,explanation:`Polenske value measures the volatile but water-insoluble fatty acids, which are abundant in coconut oil, so it rises sharply when coconut oil is added to ghee. Reichert-Meissl value measures the volatile water-soluble acids such as butyric acid that characterise genuine butterfat, while iodine and acetyl values report unsaturation and hydroxyl groups.`},{id:`q-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-14`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Saponification of 2.0 g of a fixed oil consumed 12.5 mL of 0.5 N alcoholic potassium hydroxide. Taking the equivalent weight of KOH as 56.1, the saponification value is about`,options:[`87.7 mg KOH per g`,`350 mg KOH per g`,`175 mg KOH per g`,`70.1 mg KOH per g`],answer:2,explanation:`KOH consumed = 12.5 mL x 0.5 N x 56.1 = 350.6 mg, and saponification value is expressed per gram of oil, so 350.6 / 2.0 = about 175 mg KOH per g. Quoting 350 is the usual slip, because it forgets to divide by the 2 g of oil taken.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-15`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Chaulmoogra oil, formerly used in leprosy, is characterised by fatty acids that contain`,options:[`A hydroxyl group at C-12`,`A cyclopropenoid ring`,`Three conjugated double bonds`,`A cyclopentenyl ring`],answer:3,explanation:`Chaulmoogric and hydnocarpic acids carry a cyclopentenyl ring, a feature unique among the common fixed oils. A hydroxyl at C-12 identifies ricinoleic acid of castor oil, and cyclopropenoid acids identify cottonseed oil, which is what the Halphen test detects.`},{id:`q-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-16`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Sodium alginate forms a firm gel with calcium ions chiefly because of`,options:[`Sulphate half-ester groups on the galactan chain`,`Its double-helical agarose fraction`,`Blocks of guluronic acid residues that chelate calcium in an egg-box arrangement`,`A mannose to galactose ratio of about 2 to 1`],answer:2,explanation:`Alginic acid is a copolymer of beta-D-mannuronic and alpha-L-guluronic acid, and it is the guluronic-rich blocks that hold calcium between two chains in the egg-box structure. Sulphate half-esters belong to carrageenan and agaropectin, the agarose helix to agar, and the 2 to 1 mannose to galactose ratio to guar galactomannan.`},{id:`q-pg-glycosides-9`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Baljet test, in which sodium picrate gives an orange to orange-red colour, detects`,options:[`The 2-deoxy sugar of the glycoside`,`The steroid nucleus`,`Free anthraquinone`,`The unsaturated lactone ring of the cardenolide`],answer:3,explanation:`Baljet, like Legal, Kedde and Raymond, responds to the alpha,beta-unsaturated lactone ring attached at C-17. The 2-deoxy sugar is what Keller-Kiliani detects, the steroid nucleus is what Liebermann-Burchard detects, and free anthraquinone is detected by the pink ammoniacal layer of the Borntrager test.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pg-glycosides-10`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Cascara sagrada consists of the`,options:[`Dried rhizome and root of Rheum emodi, Polygonaceae`,`Dried leaf and pod of Cassia angustifolia, Leguminosae`,`Dried bark of Rhamnus purshiana, Rhamnaceae, matured for about one year`,`Dried latex of the leaf of Aloe barbadensis, Liliaceae`],answer:2,explanation:`Cascara is the dried bark of Rhamnus purshiana, family Rhamnaceae, and it must be stored for about a year before use so that the harsh free anthrones oxidise. Rheum gives rhubarb rhizome, Cassia gives senna leaf and pod, and Aloe gives the dried leaf latex — all anthraquinone purgatives but from three other families.`},{id:`q-pg-glycosides-11`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which anthraquinone drug is the dried rhizome and root, and is astringent in small doses because it also contains appreciable tannin?`,options:[`Senna`,`Rhubarb`,`Cascara`,`Aloe`],answer:1,explanation:`Rhubarb is the dried rhizome and root of Rheum palmatum or R. emodi and contains both anthraquinones and gallotannins, so a small dose acts as an astringent while a large dose purges. Senna is a leaf and pod, cascara is a bark, and aloe is a dried latex, and none of them carries a comparable tannin content.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pg-glycosides-12`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which single test would be positive for both digitoxin and diosgenin?`,options:[`Keller-Kiliani`,`Liebermann-Burchard`,`Modified Borntrager`,`Kedde`],answer:1,explanation:`Liebermann-Burchard responds to the steroid nucleus, which digitoxin and the steroidal sapogenin diosgenin both possess, giving the blue-green colour. Keller-Kiliani needs a 2-deoxy sugar and Kedde needs the cardenolide lactone, neither of which diosgenin has, while the modified Borntrager test is for anthraquinones.`},{id:`q-pg-glycosides-13`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Scillaren A of squill differs from digitoxin of digitalis in that the aglycone of scillaren A carries`,options:[`A five-membered butenolide ring on a C23 skeleton`,`No lactone ring at all`,`A six-membered doubly unsaturated lactone on a C24 skeleton`,`An aromatic ring in place of ring D`],answer:2,explanation:`Scillaren A is a bufadienolide: a C24 steroid bearing a six-membered doubly unsaturated alpha-pyrone lactone, the same ring found in toad venom. Digitoxin is a cardenolide with a five-membered butenolide on a C23 skeleton, which is exactly the pairing the question is testing.`},{id:`q-pg-glycosides-14`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A strip of sodium picrate paper suspended over a warmed, moistened powdered drug turns brick red. This indicates`,options:[`Anthraquinone glycosides`,`Saponin glycosides`,`Cyanogenetic glycosides`,`Hydrolysable tannins`],answer:2,explanation:`Sodium picrate paper turns brick red with hydrocyanic acid released enzymatically from cyanogenetic glycosides such as amygdalin of bitter almond and prunasin of wild cherry. Anthraquinones are shown by the pink ammoniacal Borntrager layer, saponins by froth and haemolysis, and tannins by ferric chloride colours.`},{id:`q-pg-glycosides-15`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Sennosides A and B are best described as`,options:[`Dianthrone glycosides that are stereoisomers of one another`,`C-glycosides of aloe-emodin resistant to acid hydrolysis`,`Free anthraquinone aglycones of chrysophanol`,`Monoanthrone glycosides differing in the number of glucose units`],answer:0,explanation:`Both sennosides are dianthrone glycosides built from two rhein anthrone units each carrying a glucose; sennoside A is the dextrorotatory form and sennoside B the meso form, so they are stereoisomers with the same composition. The acid-resistant C-glycoside description belongs to barbaloin of aloe and to the cascarosides, not to senna.`},{id:`q-pg-glycosides-16`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which structural feature is essential for the inhibition of Na+/K+-ATPase by a cardiac glycoside?`,options:[`A 2-deoxy sugar attached at C-3`,`An aromatic ring D`,`A beta-oriented hydroxyl at C-14 with cis fusion of the A/B and C/D rings`,`A free carboxylic acid on the steroid nucleus`],answer:2,explanation:`The 14-beta-hydroxyl together with the cis-trans-cis ring fusion gives the U-shaped genin required to bind the enzyme, and epimerisation or dehydration at C-14 abolishes activity. The 2-deoxy sugar modifies solubility, potency and duration but the aglycone alone is still active, so the sugar is not essential.`},{id:`q-pg-alkaloids-9`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Opium is obtained from Papaver somniferum as the`,options:[`Dried ripe seed`,`Air-dried milky exudate of the incised unripe capsule`,`Dried root`,`Dried flowering top`],answer:1,explanation:`Opium is the air-dried latex that oozes from incisions made in the unripe capsule, and official opium contains not less than 9.5 percent anhydrous morphine. The ripe seed yields poppy seed oil and is essentially free of morphine, which is why the stage of the fruit matters so much in this definition.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pg-alkaloids-10`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Rauwolfia serpentina and Catharanthus roseus both belong to the family`,options:[`Rubiaceae`,`Solanaceae`,`Loganiaceae`,`Apocynaceae`],answer:3,explanation:`Both are Apocynaceae, and so is Strophanthus, which is why this family supplies indole alkaloids and cardiac glycosides alike. Rubiaceae supplies cinchona and ipecac, Solanaceae the tropane drugs, and Loganiaceae nux vomica.`},{id:`q-pg-alkaloids-11`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Cinchona and ipecacuanha are both Rubiaceae, but their alkaloids belong to different nuclei. Emetine and cephaeline of ipecac are`,options:[`Quinoline alkaloids`,`Indole alkaloids`,`Tropane alkaloids`,`Isoquinoline alkaloids`],answer:3,explanation:`Emetine and cephaeline are isoquinoline alkaloids, the same nucleus as morphine and papaverine, whereas quinine and cinchonine of cinchona are quinoline alkaloids. Sharing a family therefore does not mean sharing an alkaloid class, which is precisely the trap in this pair.`},{id:`q-pg-alkaloids-12`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The murexide test, which gives a purple colour on adding ammonia after evaporating the drug with an oxidising agent and acid, identifies`,options:[`Purine alkaloids`,`Tropane alkaloids`,`Ergot alkaloids`,`Steroidal alkaloids`],answer:0,explanation:`The murexide or Weidel test is the standard identification for purine alkaloids such as caffeine, theobromine and theophylline. Tropane alkaloids answer the Vitali-Morin test with its violet colour, and ergot alkaloids give the blue Van Urk colour with p-dimethylaminobenzaldehyde.`},{id:`q-pg-alkaloids-13`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Nux vomica, the source of strychnine and brucine, is the dried ripe seed of`,options:[`Physostigma venenosum, Leguminosae`,`Erythroxylum coca, Erythroxylaceae`,`Strychnos nux-vomica, Loganiaceae`,`Areca catechu, Palmae`],answer:2,explanation:`Nux vomica is the dried ripe seed of Strychnos nux-vomica, family Loganiaceae, and both its alkaloids are indole derivatives. Physostigma venenosum is the calabar bean giving physostigmine, Erythroxylum coca gives cocaine, and Areca catechu gives arecoline.`},{id:`q-pg-alkaloids-14`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Ephedrine is classified as a protoalkaloid because`,options:[`It is not derived from any amino acid`,`It is a permanently charged quaternary base`,`Its nitrogen lies outside any heterocyclic ring`,`It occurs in the plant only as a glycoside`],answer:2,explanation:`A protoalkaloid, or biological amine, has a nitrogen atom that is not part of a ring, and ephedrine, mescaline and colchicine are the standard examples. Compounds not derived from amino acids at all are the pseudoalkaloids such as caffeine and solanine, so that description belongs to a different group.`},{id:`q-pg-alkaloids-15`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Hyoscine (scopolamine) differs chemically from hyoscyamine by the presence of`,options:[`An epoxide bridge between C-6 and C-7 of the tropane ring`,`An extra methyl group on the ring nitrogen`,`Esterification with benzoic acid in place of tropic acid`,`A hydroxyl group at C-3 of the tropane ring`],answer:0,explanation:`Both alkaloids are esters of tropic acid, but hyoscine is esterified with scopine, which carries a 6,7-epoxide across the tropane ring, and this is why hyoscine is a CNS depressant while atropine is a CNS stimulant in large doses. Esterification with benzoic acid describes cocaine, not hyoscine.`},{id:`q-pg-alkaloids-16`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Cocaine is chemically an ester of ecgonine with`,options:[`Tropic acid alone`,`Benzoic acid and methyl alcohol`,`Cinnamic acid and glycerol`,`Two molecules of tropic acid`],answer:1,explanation:`Cocaine is benzoylmethylecgonine — ecgonine esterified both with benzoic acid and with methanol. Tropic acid esterified with tropine gives atropine and hyoscyamine, which is the classic point of confusion between two tropane drugs from different families.`},{id:`q-pg-volatile-oils-9`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Clove consists of the dried`,options:[`Unopened flower bud`,`Unripe fruit`,`Inner bark`,`Rhizome`],answer:0,explanation:`Clove is the dried unopened flower bud of Syzygium aromaticum, family Myrtaceae, and it yields about 15 to 20 percent of volatile oil. The over-mature fruits sold as mother cloves are a recognised adulterant, and the inner bark and rhizome descriptions belong to cinnamon and ginger respectively.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pg-volatile-oils-10`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Coriander, fennel, caraway and dill fruits all belong to the family`,options:[`Lamiaceae`,`Zingiberaceae`,`Umbelliferae`,`Myrtaceae`],answer:2,explanation:`All four are dried ripe fruits of the Umbelliferae (Apiaceae) and store their oil in schizogenous ducts called vittae. Lamiaceae supplies peppermint and thyme, Zingiberaceae supplies ginger, turmeric and cardamom, and Myrtaceae supplies clove and eucalyptus.`},{id:`q-pg-volatile-oils-11`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The chief constituent of coriander oil is`,options:[`Anethole, about 50 to 60 pc`,`Carvone, about 50 to 60 pc`,`Cineole, about 70 pc`,`Coriandrol (d-linalool), about 60 to 70 pc`],answer:3,explanation:`Coriander oil is dominated by d-linalool, called coriandrol, at roughly 60 to 70 percent. Among the other umbelliferous fruits, anethole is the marker of fennel and anise, carvone of caraway and dill, while cineole belongs to eucalyptus and cardamom.`},{id:`q-pg-volatile-oils-12`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The pungency of ginger rhizome is due to`,options:[`Zingiberene, the main sesquiterpene of its volatile oil`,`The non-volatile gingerol, which converts to the more pungent shogaol on drying`,`Curcumin`,`Cineole and terpinyl acetate`],answer:1,explanation:`Ginger owes its pungency to non-volatile phenolic ketones, chiefly gingerol, which dehydrates to the more pungent shogaol on drying and gives zingerone on further heating; the drug remains pungent after the oil is removed. Zingiberene supplies the aroma but not the bite, and curcumin is the colouring principle of turmeric.`},{id:`q-pg-volatile-oils-13`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The phenol content of clove oil is assayed by shaking the oil with potassium hydroxide solution in a`,options:[`Clevenger apparatus`,`Cassia flask`,`Soxhlet extractor`,`Dean and Stark receiver`],answer:1,explanation:`A Cassia flask has a narrow graduated neck, so after the alkali dissolves the eugenol as potassium eugenate the small volume of unabsorbed oil can be read directly. A Clevenger apparatus measures total volatile oil content by hydrodistillation, and a Soxhlet extractor is for solvent extraction, so neither measures phenol content.`},{id:`q-pg-volatile-oils-14`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Adulteration of a volatile oil with a fixed oil is detected because the adulterated oil`,options:[`Becomes turbid when shaken with water`,`Gives a red colour with ferric chloride`,`Leaves a permanent translucent stain on filter paper after warming`,`Shows a raised optical rotation with a pinene odour`],answer:2,explanation:`A genuine volatile oil evaporates completely from filter paper, so any permanent grease stain that survives warming indicates a fixed oil; the adulterated oil also fails to dissolve in the stated strength of alcohol. Turbidity with water points to added alcohol, and a raised rotation with a pinene odour points to added turpentine oil.`},{id:`q-pg-volatile-oils-15`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which pairing of a constituent with its chemical class is correct?`,options:[`Anethole — ketone`,`Carvone — phenol`,`Menthol — aldehyde`,`Cineole — oxide`],answer:3,explanation:`Cineole (eucalyptol) contains a cyclic ether oxygen, so it is classed as an oxide, alongside ascaridole of chenopodium. Anethole is a phenolic ether, carvone is a ketone and menthol is a secondary alcohol, so each of the other pairings has been shifted one class away.`},{id:`q-pg-volatile-oils-16`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which statement about menthol is correct?`,options:[`It is a sesquiterpene alcohol with two chiral centres`,`All eight stereoisomers produce the cooling sensation equally`,`Only l-menthol produces the cooling sensation, by activating the TRPM8 receptor`,`It is the principal ketone of peppermint oil`],answer:2,explanation:`Menthol is a monoterpene alcohol with three chiral centres, giving eight stereoisomers, and only the l-isomer activates the cold-sensing TRPM8 channel to produce the cooling effect. The ketone of peppermint oil is menthone, which is a separate constituent present in much smaller amount.`},{id:`q-pg-tannins-resins-9`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Nutgall, the commercial source of tannic acid, is`,options:[`The dried ripe fruit of Terminalia chebula`,`An excrescence produced by an insect on the twigs of Quercus infectoria`,`The dried aqueous extract of the heartwood of Acacia catechu`,`The dried bark of Cinchona succirubra`],answer:1,explanation:`Nutgall is a pathological excrescence formed on young twigs of Quercus infectoria, family Fagaceae, after an insect lays its egg, and it contains 50 to 70 percent gallotannic acid. Terminalia chebula fruit is myrobalan and the heartwood extract of Acacia catechu is black catechu, both tannin drugs but neither is the source of official tannic acid.`},{id:`q-pg-tannins-resins-10`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Capsaicin, the pungent principle of capsicum, is chemically`,options:[`A tropane alkaloid`,`A sesquiterpene lactone`,`A steroidal saponin`,`A vanillylamide (acid amide)`],answer:3,explanation:`Capsaicin is a vanillylamide, an acid amide of vanillylamine, and it depletes substance P from sensory C fibres, which is why repeated application desensitises the skin. It is not an alkaloid despite carrying nitrogen, and it is not a terpenoid, so capsicum is classified with the oleoresins rather than with the alkaloidal drugs.`},{id:`q-pg-tannins-resins-11`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Hydrolysis of the gallotannin of nutgall yields`,options:[`Ellagic acid and glucose`,`Gallic acid and glucose`,`Phlobaphenes and anthocyanidins`,`Catechin and rhamnose`],answer:1,explanation:`Gallotannin is a polygalloyl ester of glucose, so tannase or dilute acid liberates gallic acid and glucose. Ellagic acid with glucose comes from ellagitannins such as those of myrobalan and pomegranate rind, while red insoluble phlobaphenes are produced by boiling condensed tannins with acid.`},{id:`q-pg-tannins-resins-12`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which of the following is a true balsam?`,options:[`Canada balsam`,`Copaiba balsam`,`Balsam of Tolu`,`Colophony`],answer:2,explanation:`A true balsam must contain benzoic or cinnamic acid or their esters, and balsam of Tolu from Myroxylon balsamum qualifies, as do benzoin, Peru balsam and storax. Canada balsam and copaiba are called balsams by trade custom but are oleoresins without those acids, and colophony is a pure resin.`},{id:`q-pg-tannins-resins-13`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The Beam test, giving a violet colour with alcoholic potassium hydroxide, identifies`,options:[`Asafoetida`,`Cannabis resin`,`Benzoin`,`Colophony`],answer:1,explanation:`The Beam test is the classic colour reaction for cannabis resin. Asafoetida is identified by the Rathke test, in which ferulic acid gives a red-violet colour with sulphuric acid and alcohol, and colophony answers the Liebermann-Storch reaction with acetic anhydride and sulphuric acid.`},{id:`q-pg-tannins-resins-14`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which reagent gives a precipitate with condensed tannins but not with hydrolysable tannins?`,options:[`Bromine water`,`Dilute ferric chloride`,`Basic lead acetate`,`Gelatin solution with sodium chloride`],answer:0,explanation:`Bromine water precipitates condensed tannins because the flavan-3-ol nucleus is readily brominated, whereas hydrolysable tannins give no precipitate. Ferric chloride colours both, differing only in shade, and lead acetate and gelatin precipitate both classes, so none of those separates them.`},{id:`q-pg-tannins-resins-15`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Indian podophyllum (Podophyllum hexandrum) differs from American podophyllum (P. peltatum) in that it`,options:[`Contains no podophyllotoxin at all`,`Yields a lower resin content of about 1 to 2 percent`,`Yields a higher resin content, about 6 to 12 percent, and is richer in podophyllotoxin`,`Belongs to a different family`],answer:2,explanation:`The Indian species yields roughly 6 to 12 percent resin against about 3 to 6 percent for the American species, and its resin has the higher podophyllotoxin content, which is why it is the preferred commercial source. Both species belong to the Berberidaceae, so family is not a point of difference.`},{id:`q-pg-tannins-resins-16`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which statement about jalap resin is correct?`,options:[`It is about 90 percent soluble in alcohol, and its convolvulin fraction is insoluble in ether while jalapin is ether soluble`,`It is insoluble in alcohol but freely soluble in water`,`It is a balsamic resin containing cinnamic acid esters`,`It contains no sugar and is therefore not a glycoresin`],answer:0,explanation:`Jalap from Ipomoea purga is a glycoresin about 90 percent soluble in alcohol, and the ether-insoluble convolvulin against the ether-soluble jalapin is the standard way to separate true jalap from Indian jalap (Operculina turpethum). Being a glycoresin, its resin acids are combined with sugar, so the claim that it contains no sugar is wrong on definition.`},{id:`q-pg-extraction-isolation-9`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A violet ring at the junction of the layers when a plant extract is treated with alpha-naphthol and concentrated sulphuric acid indicates`,options:[`Alkaloids`,`Carbohydrates`,`Steroids`,`Tannins`],answer:1,explanation:`This is the Molisch test, a general test for carbohydrates, in which the acid dehydrates the sugar to furfural derivatives that condense with alpha-naphthol. Alkaloids are shown by precipitating reagents such as Mayer and Dragendorff, and steroids by the blue-green Liebermann-Burchard colour.`},{id:`q-pg-extraction-isolation-10`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Fehling and Benedict solutions in a phytochemical screen detect`,options:[`All carbohydrates including sucrose`,`Starch specifically`,`Reducing sugars only`,`Proteins and amino acids`],answer:2,explanation:`Both reagents are alkaline copper solutions reduced to red cuprous oxide by a free aldehyde or ketone group, so only reducing sugars respond; sucrose is negative until it has been hydrolysed to glucose and fructose. Molisch is the reagent that answers for all carbohydrates, and Biuret and ninhydrin cover proteins and amino acids.`},{id:`q-pg-extraction-isolation-11`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A mucilaginous drug swells into a slimy mass with the menstruum and blocks a percolator bed. The appropriate extraction process is`,options:[`Percolation with a finer powder`,`Maceration`,`Soxhlet extraction`,`Reserved percolation`],answer:1,explanation:`Maceration is chosen when the drug swells or forms a slimy mass, since the batch is simply soaked in a closed vessel with occasional shaking and nothing has to flow through a packed bed. Reducing the particle size would make the percolator block sooner, not later, so a finer powder is the opposite of the remedy.`},{id:`q-pg-extraction-isolation-12`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Counter-current extraction is favoured on a manufacturing scale because`,options:[`It needs no size reduction of the drug`,`Drug and solvent travel in opposite directions, giving a concentrated extract with minimum solvent and no applied heat`,`It requires no solvent recovery step`,`It is the standard method for volatile oils`],answer:1,explanation:`Moving the drug and solvent counter to each other keeps the most exhausted drug in contact with the freshest solvent, so a concentrated extract is obtained with a small solvent volume and without heating. It in fact demands a uniform particle size, and volatile oils are obtained by distillation or expression rather than by this method.`},{id:`q-pg-extraction-isolation-13`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A liquid extract is a preparation in which`,options:[`1 part of drug is represented in 1 part of the finished liquid`,`1 part of drug is represented in 5 parts of the finished liquid`,`1 part of drug is represented in 10 parts of the finished liquid`,`The solvent has been fully evaporated to a pilular consistency`],answer:0,explanation:`A liquid extract is concentrated so that one part by weight of the drug is represented in one part by volume of the preparation, making it the most concentrated liquid galenical. Strengths of 1 in 5 and 1 in 10 describe tinctures, and evaporation to a pilular consistency gives a soft extract.`},{id:`q-pg-extraction-isolation-14`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which stationary phase is used specifically to strip tannins from a crude plant extract before bioassay?`,options:[`Silica gel`,`Polyamide or PVPP`,`Reversed phase C18 silica`,`Strong cation exchange resin`],answer:1,explanation:`Polyamide and polyvinylpolypyrrolidone bind polyphenols through hydrogen bonding to their amide groups and are the standard way to strip tannins, which otherwise precipitate proteins and give false positives in bioassays. Sephadex LH-20 is also used, but for fractionating tannins rather than removing them wholesale. Silica gel and C18 are general separation media, and a cation exchanger targets basic compounds such as alkaloid salts.`},{id:`q-pg-extraction-isolation-15`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In the standardisation of a herbal extract, an analytical marker is`,options:[`The constituent directly responsible for the therapeutic effect`,`A characteristic constituent used only for identity and quantitation, whether or not it contributes to activity`,`Any impurity limited by the monograph`,`The residual solvent measured after drying`],answer:1,explanation:`An analytical marker is chosen because it is characteristic and measurable, and it fixes identity and batch consistency without any claim about activity. The constituent responsible for the effect, such as the sennosides of senna, is called the active marker, and confusing the two is the standard error here.`},{id:`q-pg-extraction-isolation-16`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Three successive 100 mL portions of solvent extract more of a constituent than a single 300 mL portion because`,options:[`The smaller volume raises the temperature of extraction`,`Each fresh portion restores the concentration gradient, so the partition equilibrium is re-established repeatedly`,`The partition coefficient of the constituent increases with each extraction`,`The marc swells less in a smaller volume of solvent`],answer:1,explanation:`Extraction is governed by a partition equilibrium between menstruum and marc, and each fresh portion of solvent starts again at zero concentration, so the drug is depleted step by step; this is the same principle that makes percolation more efficient than maceration. The partition coefficient is a constant of the system and does not change from one extraction to the next.`},{id:`q-pg-fibres-adulteration-9`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Under the microscope, cotton fibre appears as`,options:[`A flattened, twisted, ribbon-like unicellular hair with a lumen`,`A bundle of lignified multicellular fibres with cross markings`,`A smooth structureless rod without a lumen`,`A fibre with an overlapping scaly cuticle`],answer:0,explanation:`Cotton is a single epidermal hair of the seed of Gossypium species, so it appears as a flattened twisted ribbon with thick walls and a distinct lumen, and it is negative to lignin tests. Lignified multicellular bundles describe jute, a structureless rod describes silk, and the scaly cuticle belongs to wool.`,tag:`Structure ID`},{id:`q-pg-fibres-adulteration-10`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Argel leaf (Solenostemma argel) is a well-known adulterant of`,options:[`Digitalis leaf`,`Senna leaf`,`Belladonna leaf`,`Clove`],answer:1,explanation:`Argel leaves, which are thicker, greyish-green and wrinkled with a distinct bitter taste, are the classic adulterant of senna and are detected by microscopy and by the leaf constants. Digitalis is adulterated with Verbascum and Digitalis thapsi, and clove with exhausted cloves and clove stalks.`},{id:`q-pg-fibres-adulteration-11`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Exhausted (deoiled) clove mixed into a consignment is recognised because it`,options:[`Gives a much darker colour with ferric chloride`,`Yields far less volatile oil and floats horizontally in water instead of sinking or floating vertically`,`Shows lignified fibres absent from genuine clove`,`Has a markedly higher acid-insoluble ash`],answer:1,explanation:`Deoiled clove is lighter and less dense than the genuine drug, so it lies horizontally on water while a sound clove sinks or floats with the bud end upwards, and the Clevenger assay confirms the low oil content. Acid-insoluble ash rises with added sand and earth, which is a different type of adulteration altogether.`},{id:`q-pg-fibres-adulteration-12`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Supplying carved wooden nutmegs in place of genuine nutmeg is an example of`,options:[`An inferior commercial variety`,`An exhausted drug`,`An artificially manufactured substitute`,`Addition of worthless heavy material`],answer:2,explanation:`A fabricated imitation shaped to resemble the drug falls under artificially manufactured substitutes, and it is exposed at once by microscopy and by the absence of the volatile oil. An exhausted drug still has genuine tissue, and added sand or stones would be classed as worthless heavy material.`},{id:`q-pg-fibres-adulteration-13`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Plain catgut is absorbed in about 7 to 10 days. Chromicised catgut lasts about`,options:[`1 to 2 days`,`20 to 40 days`,`6 months`,`It is never absorbed`],answer:1,explanation:`Tanning the collagen with chromic salts slows enzymatic digestion, extending absorption to roughly 20 to 40 days, which is why it is used where tissue support is needed for longer. Catgut is always absorbable, so the option that it is never absorbed contradicts the definition of the suture.`},{id:`q-pg-fibres-adulteration-14`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In the USP numbering of sutures, a 5-0 thread compared with a 2-0 thread is`,options:[`Thicker`,`Finer`,`Of the same diameter but stronger`,`Always absorbable`],answer:1,explanation:`The more zeros in the designation, the finer the thread, so 5-0 is considerably thinner than 2-0 and is used where delicate tissue apposition is required. The numbering describes diameter only and says nothing about whether the material is absorbable.`},{id:`q-pg-fibres-adulteration-15`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The first plant cell culture product to be produced commercially was`,options:[`Berberine from Coptis japonica`,`Paclitaxel from Taxus species`,`Ginsenosides from Panax ginseng`,`Shikonin from Lithospermum erythrorhizon`],answer:3,explanation:`Shikonin, a red naphthoquinone pigment produced from Lithospermum erythrorhizon suspension culture, was the first commercially successful plant cell culture product. Berberine from Coptis, ginsenosides from Panax root culture and paclitaxel by plant cell fermentation all followed later.`},{id:`q-pg-fibres-adulteration-16`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Hairy root cultures, valued for their fast growth and stable secondary metabolite yield, are induced by infection with`,options:[`Agrobacterium rhizogenes`,`Agrobacterium tumefaciens`,`A medium containing only cytokinin`,`Claviceps purpurea`],answer:0,explanation:`Agrobacterium rhizogenes transfers its Ri plasmid and produces the fast-growing, plagiotropic hairy roots that synthesise root-specific metabolites without added hormones. Agrobacterium tumefaciens carries the Ti plasmid and causes crown gall, an undifferentiated tumour, and a cytokinin-rich medium favours shoots rather than roots.`}],n=[{id:`f-pg-classification-evaluation-1`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,front:`Which ash value detects sand and earthy adulteration?`,back:`Acid-insoluble ash — silica does not dissolve in dilute HCl.`},{id:`f-pg-classification-evaluation-2`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,front:`Stomatal index formula?`,back:`Stomatal index = [S / (E + S)] x 100, where S = stomata and E = epidermal cells in the same area.`},{id:`f-pg-classification-evaluation-3`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,front:`Vein-islet number of Alexandrian versus Tinnevelly senna?`,back:`Alexandrian (Cassia acutifolia) about 25.0 to 29.5; Tinnevelly (C. angustifolia) about 19.5 to 22.5.`},{id:`f-pg-classification-evaluation-4`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,front:`Stomatal type of belladonna, datura and hyoscyamus?`,back:`Anisocytic (cruciferous) — the Solanaceae pattern.`},{id:`f-pg-classification-evaluation-5`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,front:`Bitterness value reference standard?`,back:`Quinine hydrochloride, 1 g in 2000 mL of water.`},{id:`f-pg-classification-evaluation-6`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,front:`Swelling index of ispaghula husk?`,back:`Not less than 40 — the highest among official drugs.`},{id:`f-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-1`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,front:`Biological source and family of acacia?`,back:`Acacia senegal, Leguminosae — dried gummy exudate of stem and branches.`},{id:`f-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-2`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,front:`Acacia versus tragacanth in water?`,back:`Acacia dissolves completely (emulsifier); tragacanth only swells into a stiff gel (suspending agent).`},{id:`f-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-3`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,front:`Which starch has the largest granules with an eccentric hilum?`,back:`Potato starch — ovoid, up to about 100 micrometres, with concentric striations.`},{id:`f-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-4`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,front:`Ester value equation?`,back:`Ester value = saponification value - acid value (all as mg KOH per g of oil).`},{id:`f-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-5`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,front:`Baudouin, Bellier and Halphen tests detect which oils?`,back:`Baudouin — sesame; Bellier — arachis; Halphen — cottonseed.`},{id:`f-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-6`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,front:`Which algae give agar and which give alginates?`,back:`Agar and carrageenan from red algae (Rhodophyceae); alginates from brown algae (Phaeophyceae).`},{id:`f-pg-glycosides-1`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,front:`Which part of a cardiac glycoside does each test detect: Keller-Kiliani, Kedde, Liebermann-Burchard?`,back:`Keller-Kiliani — 2-deoxy sugar; Kedde — five-membered unsaturated lactone; Liebermann-Burchard — steroid nucleus.`},{id:`f-pg-glycosides-2`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,front:`Why does aloe need the modified Borntrager test?`,back:`Barbaloin is a C-glycoside in the reduced anthrone form; ferric chloride oxidises it to anthraquinone first.`},{id:`f-pg-glycosides-3`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,front:`Cardenolide versus bufadienolide?`,back:`Cardenolide: C23, five-membered lactone (digoxin). Bufadienolide: C24, six-membered lactone (scillaren A).`},{id:`f-pg-glycosides-4`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,front:`Biological source of strophanthus and its chief glycoside?`,back:`Strophanthus kombe / S. gratus, Apocynaceae — K-strophanthoside and ouabain from the ripe seed.`},{id:`f-pg-glycosides-5`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,front:`Two tests for saponins?`,back:`Foam test (persistent honeycomb froth) and haemolysis test (lysis of red blood cells).`},{id:`f-pg-glycosides-6`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,front:`Glycoside and enzyme of black mustard, and the product?`,back:`Sinigrin + myrosin give volatile allyl isothiocyanate — the vesicant of Brassica nigra.`},{id:`f-pg-alkaloids-1`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,front:`Composition of Mayer, Dragendorff, Wagner and Hager reagents?`,back:`Mayer — potassium mercuric iodide; Dragendorff — potassium bismuth iodide; Wagner — iodine in KI; Hager — picric acid.`},{id:`f-pg-alkaloids-2`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,front:`Test that identifies opium?`,back:`Meconic acid test — deep red with ferric chloride, not discharged by hydrochloric acid.`},{id:`f-pg-alkaloids-3`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,front:`Thalleioquin test detects what, and why not cinchonine?`,back:`Quinine (emerald green with bromine water then ammonia); cinchonine lacks the 6-methoxy group.`},{id:`f-pg-alkaloids-4`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,front:`Biological source of ergot and its nucleus?`,back:`Claviceps purpurea sclerotium on Secale cereale (rye); indole alkaloids based on lysergic acid.`},{id:`f-pg-alkaloids-5`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,front:`Nucleus of emetine, reserpine and pilocarpine?`,back:`Emetine — isoquinoline; reserpine — indole; pilocarpine — imidazole.`},{id:`f-pg-alkaloids-6`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,front:`Vitali-Morin test detects?`,back:`Tropane alkaloids — fuming nitric acid then alcoholic KOH gives a violet colour.`},{id:`f-pg-volatile-oils-1`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,front:`Carbon count: mono, sesqui, di, tri, tetraterpene?`,back:`C10, C15, C20, C30, C40 respectively.`},{id:`f-pg-volatile-oils-2`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,front:`Chief constituent of clove, cinnamon, peppermint and eucalyptus oils?`,back:`Eugenol; cinnamaldehyde; menthol; cineole.`},{id:`f-pg-volatile-oils-3`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,front:`Why are citrus peel oils expressed rather than distilled?`,back:`Their aldehydes such as citral are heat sensitive and distillation destroys the aroma.`},{id:`f-pg-volatile-oils-4`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,front:`Which volatile oils are heavier than water?`,back:`Clove, cinnamon and cassia oils — because of their high phenol and aldehyde content.`},{id:`f-pg-volatile-oils-5`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,front:`Chief constituent of chenopodium oil, and its hazard?`,back:`Ascaridole, a peroxide monoterpene — explosive if heated with organic acids.`},{id:`f-pg-volatile-oils-6`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,front:`How is volatile oil content of a crude drug determined?`,back:`Hydrodistillation in a Clevenger apparatus, expressed as percent v/w.`},{id:`f-pg-tannins-resins-1`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,front:`Ferric chloride colour: hydrolysable versus condensed tannins?`,back:`Hydrolysable — blue to blue-black; condensed — green to green-black.`},{id:`f-pg-tannins-resins-2`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,front:`What does the goldbeater skin test distinguish?`,back:`True tannins (positive, brown-black stain) from pseudotannins such as gallic acid and catechin (negative).`},{id:`f-pg-tannins-resins-3`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,front:`Define oleoresin, oleo-gum-resin and balsam with one example each.`,back:`Oleoresin — resin + volatile oil (ginger); oleo-gum-resin — plus gum (asafoetida); balsam — resin with benzoic or cinnamic acid (benzoin).`},{id:`f-pg-tannins-resins-4`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,front:`Source and active of podophyllum?`,back:`Podophyllum hexandrum / P. peltatum, Berberidaceae — podophyllotoxin, a lignan; parent of etoposide.`},{id:`f-pg-tannins-resins-5`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,front:`Sumatra versus Siam benzoin?`,back:`Sumatra (Styrax benzoin) has cinnamic and benzoic acids; Siam (S. tonkinensis) has essentially only benzoic acid.`},{id:`f-pg-tannins-resins-6`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,front:`What is colophony and its chief constituent?`,back:`The non-volatile residue after distilling turpentine from Pinus oleoresin; abietic acid.`},{id:`f-pg-extraction-isolation-1`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,front:`Menstruum, marc and miscella?`,back:`Menstruum = solvent; marc = exhausted drug residue; miscella = solvent with dissolved constituents.`},{id:`f-pg-extraction-isolation-2`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,front:`Why is percolation used for potent tinctures?`,back:`Fresh solvent continuously contacts the drug bed, so extraction is nearly complete unlike batch maceration.`},{id:`f-pg-extraction-isolation-3`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,front:`Critical temperature and pressure of CO2?`,back:`About 31.1 degrees C and 73.8 bar — low enough for thermolabile constituents.`},{id:`f-pg-extraction-isolation-4`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,front:`Screening tests for flavonoids and for steroids?`,back:`Flavonoids — Shinoda (Mg + conc. HCl, pink-magenta); steroids — Liebermann-Burchard (blue-green) and Salkowski (red lower layer).`},{id:`f-pg-extraction-isolation-5`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,front:`Solvent order for successive fractionation?`,back:`Petroleum ether, chloroform, ethyl acetate, n-butanol, then water — increasing polarity.`},{id:`f-pg-extraction-isolation-6`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,front:`Molisch test — reagent and result?`,back:`Alpha-naphthol in alcohol with conc. sulphuric acid layered below; violet ring means carbohydrate.`},{id:`f-pg-fibres-adulteration-1`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,front:`Test that detects jute in a cotton dressing?`,back:`Phloroglucinol with conc. HCl gives a pink-red colour with lignified jute; cotton is negative.`},{id:`f-pg-fibres-adulteration-2`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,front:`Single test separating wool from silk?`,back:`Lead acetate with alkali — wool keratin contains sulphur and gives black lead sulphide; silk does not.`},{id:`f-pg-fibres-adulteration-3`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,front:`Sinking time requirement for absorbent cotton?`,back:`It must sink within 10 seconds when dropped on water.`},{id:`f-pg-fibres-adulteration-4`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,front:`Five types of adulteration?`,back:`Inferior commercial variety, superficially similar substance, exhausted drug, artificial substitute, addition of worthless heavy material.`},{id:`f-pg-fibres-adulteration-5`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,front:`How is an exhausted drug detected?`,back:`Low alcohol- and water-soluble extractive values and low water-soluble ash, with normal microscopy.`},{id:`f-pg-fibres-adulteration-6`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,front:`Marine sources of cytarabine and ziconotide?`,back:`Cytarabine from the sponge Cryptotethya crypta; ziconotide from the cone snail Conus magus.`},{id:`f-pg-classification-evaluation-7`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,front:`Foaming index formula?`,back:`Foaming index = 1000 / a, where a is the volume in mL of decoction in the tube showing 1 cm of persistent foam.`},{id:`f-pg-classification-evaluation-8`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,front:`Palisade ratio versus vein-islet number?`,back:`Palisade ratio: average palisade cells under one epidermal cell. Vein-islet number: vein-islets per square millimetre of lamina.`},{id:`f-pg-classification-evaluation-9`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,front:`Bioassay methods for digitalis, insulin and vitamin D?`,back:`Digitalis — pigeon emetic method; insulin — rabbit blood sugar or mouse convulsion; vitamin D — rat line test.`},{id:`f-pg-classification-evaluation-10`,topicId:`pg-classification-evaluation`,front:`Which loci are used for plant DNA barcoding?`,back:`rbcL, matK, ITS2 and psbA-trnH. COI is the animal barcode, 16S rRNA the bacterial one.`},{id:`f-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-7`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,front:`Chief ester of beeswax, carnauba wax and spermaceti?`,back:`Beeswax — myricyl palmitate; carnauba — myricyl cerotate; spermaceti — cetyl palmitate.`},{id:`f-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-8`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,front:`Which constant detects rancidity and which detects unsaturation?`,back:`Acid value detects rancidity (free fatty acids); iodine value measures unsaturation.`},{id:`f-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-9`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,front:`Reichert-Meissl versus Polenske value?`,back:`Reichert-Meissl — volatile water-soluble acids (butyric type, butter and ghee); Polenske — volatile water-insoluble acids, detects coconut oil in ghee.`},{id:`f-pg-carbohydrates-lipids-10`,topicId:`pg-carbohydrates-lipids`,front:`Source, family and part used of isabgol and guar gum?`,back:`Isabgol — Plantago ovata, Plantaginaceae, seed husk. Guar gum — Cyamopsis tetragonoloba, Leguminosae, seed endosperm.`},{id:`f-pg-glycosides-7`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,front:`What do Baljet, Legal, Kedde and Raymond tests all detect?`,back:`The alpha,beta-unsaturated lactone ring of cardiac glycosides — not the sugar and not the steroid nucleus.`},{id:`f-pg-glycosides-8`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,front:`Source, family and part used of cascara and rhubarb?`,back:`Cascara — Rhamnus purshiana, Rhamnaceae, bark aged about one year. Rhubarb — Rheum palmatum / R. emodi, Polygonaceae, rhizome and root.`},{id:`f-pg-glycosides-9`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,front:`Chemical type of sennosides A and B?`,back:`Dianthrone glycosides of rhein anthrone; sennoside A is dextrorotatory and sennoside B is the meso isomer.`},{id:`f-pg-glycosides-10`,topicId:`pg-glycosides`,front:`Primary versus secondary glycoside in Digitalis purpurea?`,back:`Primary — purpurea glycoside A in the living leaf; secondary — digitoxin, formed when digipurpidase removes glucose during drying.`},{id:`f-pg-alkaloids-7`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,front:`Family of Rauwolfia, Catharanthus and Strophanthus?`,back:`All Apocynaceae.`},{id:`f-pg-alkaloids-8`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,front:`Cinchona and ipecac are both Rubiaceae — what alkaloid nucleus does each have?`,back:`Cinchona — quinoline (quinine, quinidine). Ipecac — isoquinoline (emetine, cephaeline).`},{id:`f-pg-alkaloids-9`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,front:`Murexide test and Van Urk test detect what?`,back:`Murexide — purine alkaloids (caffeine, theobromine). Van Urk (p-DMAB) — ergot and other indole alkaloids, blue colour.`},{id:`f-pg-alkaloids-10`,topicId:`pg-alkaloids`,front:`Part used for opium and its official morphine content?`,back:`Air-dried latex of the incised unripe capsule of Papaver somniferum; not less than 9.5 percent anhydrous morphine.`},{id:`f-pg-volatile-oils-7`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,front:`Part used: clove, cinnamon, ginger, cardamom?`,back:`Clove — dried unopened flower bud; cinnamon — dried inner bark; ginger — dried rhizome; cardamom — dried ripe fruit and seed.`},{id:`f-pg-volatile-oils-8`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,front:`Chief constituent of coriander, fennel and caraway oils?`,back:`Coriander — coriandrol (d-linalool) 60-70 pc; fennel — anethole 50-60 pc; caraway — carvone 50-60 pc.`},{id:`f-pg-volatile-oils-9`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,front:`How is the eugenol content of clove oil assayed?`,back:`Shake with potassium hydroxide solution in a Cassia flask and read the unabsorbed oil in the graduated neck.`},{id:`f-pg-volatile-oils-10`,topicId:`pg-volatile-oils`,front:`Family of clove and eucalyptus; of cinnamon and cassia?`,back:`Clove and eucalyptus — Myrtaceae. Cinnamon and cassia — Lauraceae.`},{id:`f-pg-tannins-resins-7`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,front:`Hydrolysis products of gallitannin versus ellagitannin?`,back:`Gallitannin — gallic acid + glucose (nutgall). Ellagitannin — ellagic acid + glucose (myrobalan, pomegranate rind).`},{id:`f-pg-tannins-resins-8`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,front:`Which reagent precipitates condensed tannins but not hydrolysable ones?`,back:`Bromine water.`},{id:`f-pg-tannins-resins-9`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,front:`Beam test and Rathke test identify what?`,back:`Beam — cannabis resin (violet with alcoholic KOH). Rathke — asafoetida (red-violet from ferulic acid).`},{id:`f-pg-tannins-resins-10`,topicId:`pg-tannins-resins`,front:`Capsaicin — chemical class and mechanism?`,back:`A vanillylamide (acid amide), not a terpene or alkaloid; it depletes substance P from sensory C fibres.`},{id:`f-pg-extraction-isolation-7`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,front:`Which adsorbents remove tannins from a crude extract?`,back:`Sephadex LH-20 and polyamide.`},{id:`f-pg-extraction-isolation-8`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,front:`Active marker versus analytical marker?`,back:`Active marker — the constituent responsible for the effect (sennosides in senna). Analytical marker — characteristic constituent used only for identity and quantitation.`},{id:`f-pg-extraction-isolation-9`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,front:`What do Fehling and Benedict solutions detect, and why is sucrose negative?`,back:`Reducing sugars only; sucrose has no free aldehyde or ketone group until it is hydrolysed.`},{id:`f-pg-extraction-isolation-10`,topicId:`pg-extraction-isolation`,front:`Strength of a tincture versus a liquid extract?`,back:`Tincture — usually 1 part drug in 5 or 10 parts. Liquid extract — 1 part drug represented in 1 part of the liquid.`},{id:`f-pg-fibres-adulteration-7`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,front:`Classic adulterants of senna leaf and of saffron?`,back:`Senna — argel leaf (Solenostemma argel), Dodonaea, Cassia obovata. Saffron — dried florets of Carthamus tinctorius.`},{id:`f-pg-fibres-adulteration-8`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,front:`How is exhausted (deoiled) clove recognised?`,back:`It floats horizontally in water and yields far less volatile oil; genuine clove sinks or floats vertically.`},{id:`f-pg-fibres-adulteration-9`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,front:`Absorption time of plain versus chromicised catgut?`,back:`Plain about 7 to 10 days; chromicised about 20 to 40 days.`},{id:`f-pg-fibres-adulteration-10`,topicId:`pg-fibres-adulteration`,front:`First commercial plant cell culture product, and which bacterium gives hairy roots?`,back:`Shikonin from Lithospermum erythrorhizon; hairy roots are induced by Agrobacterium rhizogenes (Ri plasmid).`}];export{n as flashcards,e as notes,t as questions};